Although Save the Children is preparing a team it will send to Palu, they still don’t know if foreign staff can work on the ground.
Mr Sutopo, a spokesman for the national emergency agency, explained that the authorities were assessing the situation in Palu to see if international organizations were allowed to provide assistance.
Considering that the land is constantly moving in Indonesia, the country remains incredibly unprepared when nature unleashes its anger.
Although tsunami shelters have been built in Aceh, there are not so many in other coastal areas.
It is likely that the apparent lack of tsunami warning sirens in Palu, even though there was a previous warning, has contributed to the loss of life.
At best, traveling from one island of Indonesia to another is a challenge.
Natural disasters further complicate logistics.
A hospital ship in Lombok to care for the victims of the quake is heading to Palu, but it will take at least three days to reach the enclave of the new disaster.
President Joko Widodo made improving Indonesia’s worn-out infrastructure at the helm of his election campaign, and has allocated quite a bit of money to roads and rail lines.
However, the lack of funding has affected Mr. Joko's administration, which faces re-election next year.
In addition, it must face pressure from persistent sectarian tensions in Indonesia, where members of the Muslim majority have adopted a more conservative form of faith.
More than 1000 people died and tens of thousands had to leave their homes while gangs of Christians and Muslims fought in the streets wearing machetes, bows and arrows, and other rudimentary weapons.
Look at this: Daniel Sturridge, from Liverpool, matches the scoring against Chelsea
Daniel Sturridge saved Liverpool from a Premier League loss to Chelsea with an 89th-minute goal on Saturday at London’s Stamford Bridge.
Sturridge received a pass from Xherdan Shaqiri just over 27 metres from Chelsea’s goal when the score was 1-0.
He placed the ball in his left foot with a touch before throwing it to the second stick.
The action commanded it above the area and headed towards the upper right corner of the network.
Finally, the ball went over Kepa Arrizabalaga who tried to stop it with a jump and fell into the net.
