However, the Cromwell Society described Mr. Crick’s suggestions as a “madness” and an “attempt to rewrite history.”
John Goldsmith, president of the Cromwell Society, commented: “In today’s debate on the removal of statues, it was inevitable that Oliver Cromwell’s figure would become a target.
The iconoclasm of the English civil wars was not ordered or carried out by Cromwell.
The wrong Cromwell may be sacrificed for the actions of his ancestor, Thomas, during the previous century.
Cromwell’s magnificent portrayal of Sir William Hamo Thorneycroft is proof of 19th-century opinion and part of the historiography of a figure that many deem worthy to celebrate.
As Mr. Goldsmith pointed out to The Sunday Telegraph: “Many consider Cromwell, perhaps more at the end of the 19th century than at present, a defender of parliament in the face of external pressure, in this case, it is clear the monarchy.
