Killed Pig Attacks Chinese Farmer
A pig attacked and killed a rancher in a market in southwestern China, according to local media.
The man, identified solely by his surname “Yuan,” was found dead with an artery sectioned and covered in blood near a pigsty from the Liupanshui market in Guizhou province, as the South China Morning Post reported Sunday.
On May 30, 2005, a pig breeder prepares to inject pigs from a pocylligue in Xining in Qinghai (China).
He reportedly traveled with his cousin from neighboring Yunnan province on Wednesday to sell 15 pigs at the market.
The next morning, his cousin found him dead and discovered that the door of a nearby pocylligan was open.
He said that in the pigsty there was a large pig with blood in his mouth.
Forensic examination confirmed that the almost 250-kilo pork had attacked the farmer to death, according to the report.
“My cousin’s legs were bloodied and shattered,” the cousin, who is addressed by his surname, “Wu,” told The Guiyang Evening News.
Security camera footage showed Yuan entering the market on Thursday at 4:40 to feed his pigs.
His body was found about an hour later.
The animal that killed the man belonged neither to Yuan nor his cousin.
A market manager told The Evening News that the pig was locked up to prevent him from attacking anyone else, while police collected evidence at the scene.
It appears that Yuan's family and market authorities are trading compensation for his death.
Though they're rare, it's not the first time a pig attacked a person.
In 2016, a pig attacked a woman and her husband on her Massachusetts farm, leaving the man seriously injured.
Ten years earlier, an almost 295-kilo pig cornered a farmer in his tractor until his wife frightened the animal.
After, in 2012, pigs ate an Oregon rancher, a Manitoba farmer told CBC News that pigs aren’t usually aggressive, but that the taste of blood can act as a “trigger.”
"They're just playing.
They're like children, very curious... their intention isn't to hurt.
“What you have to do is have their proper respect,” he said.
The remnants of Hurricane Rosa will leave widespread heavy rains in southwestern U.S.
As planned, Hurricane Rosa is weakening as it moves over the cooler waters off Mexico’s northern coast.
However, Rosa will leave torrential rains throughout northern Mexico and southwestern U.S. in the coming days.
Rosa caused wind gusts of more than 136 km/h, a Category 1 hurricane, from Sunday at five in the morning (Eastern time) and was located almost 620 kilometers southwest of Punta Eugenia (Mexico).
Rosa is expected to move north on Sunday.
Meanwhile, low pressures are beginning in the Pacific Ocean, which are shifting eastbound toward the U.S. West Coast as Rosa approaches the Baja California peninsula on Monday, a tropical storm will begin to boost heavy tropical rains northbound toward the U.S. southwestern U.S.
Rosa will leave more than 25 centimeters of rain in some parts of Mexico on Monday.
Finally, as tropical rain collides with the approaching low pressure, there will be widespread heavy rainfall in the Southwest over the next few days.
Locally, between 2 and 10 centimeters of rain will cause dangerous flash flooding, debris flows and possible detachments in the desert.
Intense tropical rains will cause rainfall of nearly 5 to 8 centimeters per hour in specific areas, especially in parts of southern Nevada and Arizona.
Between 5 and 10 centimeters of rain are expected in parts of the southwest, especially in most of Arizona.
Due to the scattered nature of tropical rains, there may be flash floods with rapidly worsening conditions.
Due to this threat of torrential tropical rains, it is not recommended at all to venture into the desert on foot.
Heavy rains could turn canyons into raging rivers and storms will cause gusty winds and dust clouds locally.
Closing low pressures will leave heavy rain in concrete parts of the coast of Southern California.
Torrential rains may leave up to almost two centimeters of water, which could cause small flows of debris and make roads slippery.
It would be the first torrential rainfall in the region during the wet season.
Some scattered tropical showers will begin to approach Arizona late Sunday and early Monday, before the rain generalizes late Monday and Tuesday.
Heavy rains will spread to the Four Corners on Tuesday and throughout Wednesday.
In October there may be some intense temperature changes in the U.S. due to Arctic cooling and the warmth of the tropics.
Sometimes this involves drastic temperature changes over short distances.
A good example of these temperature differences is what will happen throughout the U.S. center on Sunday.
There will be a nearly 7-degree difference between Kansas City, Missouri and Omaha (Nebraska), and between St. Louis and Des Moines (Iowa).
Over the next few days, prolonged summer heat will try to recover and re-expand.
In a large part of central and eastern U.S., a warm October start is expected, with generalized temperatures around 26 degrees from the Southern Plain to parts of the northeast.
On Tuesday, in New York temperatures of up to 26 degrees could be reached, about 12 degrees above average.
Our long-term forecast indicates high odds of above-average temperatures for the eastern U.S. during the first half of October.
More than 20 million people watched Brett Kavanaugh
More than 20 million people followed the heartbreaking testimonies Brett Kavanaugh, a Supreme Court candidate, and Christine Blasey Ford, the woman who accused him of a sexual assault presumed in the 1980s, gave Thursday on six television networks.
Meanwhile, the policy continued to stalemate and the media interrupted its usual programming to broadcast Friday’s latest developments: an agreement designed by Arizona Senator Jeff Blake for the FBI to carry out an investigation into the allegations for a week.
Ford informed the Senate Judiciary Committee that he is absolutely certain that Kavanaugh robbed her drunk and that he tried to take out his clothes at a high school party.
During his passionate testimony, Kavanaugh said he was totally sure that had not happened.
It’s likely that more than the 20.4 million Nielsen commented on Friday have seen it.
The company counted the average audience on CBS, ABC, NBC, CNN, Fox News Channel and MSNBC.
The numbers were not immediately available for other channels that relayed it, such as PBS, C-SPAN and Fox Business Network.
In addition, Nielsen usually has some trouble calculating how many people see something in the office.
To put it in perspective, it’s an audience similar to that of a playoff football game or the Oscars.
Fox News Channel, whose tertullians have vigorously supported Kavanaugh’s designation, the leading channel, with an average of 5.69 million viewers during an all-day appearance, Nielsen said.
ABC ranked second with 3.26 million viewers.
CBS had 3.1 million, NCB had 2.94 million, MSNBC had 2.89 million, and CNN had 2.52 million, Nielsen said.
After the appearance, there was still great interest.
Flake was the central character of Friday's drama.
After the moderate Republican’s office issued a statement that it would vote for Kavanaugh, on Friday morning CNN and CSB chambers caught protesters yelling at him while trying to get on an elevator to attend an appearance before the Judiciary Commission.
While receiving these reproaches, broadcast live by CNN, he was dejected.
“Look me in the face,” one woman said.
“Do you think she’s telling the truth to the country?”
“You have power when there are a lot of women who don’t.”
Flake said his office had issued a statement and, before the elevator doors closed, that he would tell more in his appearance before the Commission.
Cable television and broadcasting networks broadcast it all live later, when the Judiciary Committee had to vote to propose Kavanaugh to the Senate to be voted.
However, Flake said he would only do so if the FBI was investigating the allegations made against the candidate over the next week, which some Democrats had been asking for.
Flake was convinced, in part because of the conversations he had had with his friend, Democratic Senator Chris Coons.
After a conversation with Coons and several senators later, Flake made his decision.
Flake’s decision was pivotal as it was clear that Republicans wouldn’t have enough votes to approve Kavanaugh without such an investigation.
President Trump has opened an FBI investigation into allegations against Kavanaugh.
British Prime Minister May accuses critics of ‘doing politics’ with Brexit
Prime Minister Theresa May accused critics of her plan to leave the European Union to “do politics” with Britain’s future and to undermine national interest in an interview with the Sunday Times.
British Prime Minister Theresa May arrives Congress of the Conservative Party to be held in Birmingham (Britain) on September 29, 2018.
