Wayde Murder: Police Arrest Dyteon Simpson Suspeck of LSU Player's Death
Police have arrested a suspect in the death of Wayde Sims, a 20-year-old LSU basketball player, during a shooting.
Dyteon Simpson, 20, has been arrested and jailed on a second-degree manslaughter charge, according to the Baton Rouge Police Department.
Authorities released a video of the confrontation between Sims and Simpson, and police say Sims lost his glasses during the fight.
Police regained their glasses at the crime scene and said they had found Simpson's DNA on them, CBS affiliate WAFB reports.
After interrogating Simpson, police say he admitted to shooting Wayde.
A bond of 350 000 USD has been established, according to the defense.
Baton Rouge Eastern District Investigating Court released a preliminary report Friday stating that the cause of death had been a gunshot wound to the back of its head.
The department recognizes the merits of the Louisiana State Police fugitives unit, the State Police crime lab, the Southern University police and citizens for their assistance in the investigation that led to the arrest.
Joe Alleva, LSU’s sporting director, thanked police forces in the area for their “diligence and quest for justice.”
Sims was 20 years old.
The player, who measured more than two meters, grew up in Baton Rouge, where his father, Wayne, also played basketball at LSU.
He had scored an average of 5.6 points and 2.6 rebounds in a game of the last season.
On Friday morning, LSU basketball coach Will Wade stated that his team is “devastated” and “shocked” by Wayde’s death.
“These are the things that people always care about,” Wade said.
Volcano throws ash in Mexico City
Ash from the Popocatepetl volcano has reached the neighborhoods located south of the capital of Mexico.
The National Disaster Prevention Center on Saturday warned Mexicans to stay away from the volcano after the crater showed activity and recorded 183 emissions of gas and ash in 24 hours.
The center recorded several tremors and shakes.
On social networks, the images showed thin layers of ash covering the windshields of vehicles in neighborhoods of Mexico City, such as Xochimilco.
Geophysicists have observed increased activity in the volcano, located 72 kilometers southeast of the capital, since a 7.1-magnitude quake struck central Mexico in September 2017.
The volcano, known as “Don Goyo” has been active since 1994.
Police Face Catalan Separatists Ahead of Independence Referendum Anniversary
Six people were arrested Saturday in Barcelona after pro-independence protesters clashed with anti-riots as thousands participated in opposition demonstrations coinciding with the first anniversary of Catalonia’s polarized referendum on secession.
A group of hooded separatists to whom the riot rioters had cut through threw eggs and powder paint, creating dark clouds of dust in the streets, which are normally full of tourists.
Clashes also occurred in later hours and the police used batons to contain the fight.
For several hours, pro-independence groups, with messages such as “neither forgetting nor pardon”, confronted unionist protesters to the cry of “Viva España”.
Fourteen people were treated for minor injuries sustained during the protests, according to local media.
Tensions continue to be high in this region a year after the October 1 vote, considered illegal by Madrid, but celebrated by separatist Catalans.
Voters voted an overwhelming majority for independence, though turnout was low, as people who were against secession largely boycotted the referendum.
According to the Catalan authorities, almost 1000 people were injured last year after the attempt of the police to prevent the vote at polling stations throughout the region, where violent clashes occurred.
Pro-independence groups camped out Friday night to avoid a demonstration in support of the National Police.
The demonstration went ahead, but was forced to take a different route.
Narcis Termes, 68, an electricist attending the separatist protest with his wife, said he was no longer hopeful that Catalonia would become independent.
“Last year we experienced one of our best moments.
“I saw my parents crying with joy for being able to vote, but now we’re stuck,” he said.
Despite achieving a vital victory, albeit narrow, in last December’s regional elections, pro-Catalanist parties have struggled to retain momentum this year, as many of their best-known leaders, whether in self-imposed or detained exile, are awaiting trial for their role in organizing the referendum and the subsequent declaration of independence.
Puig, a 42-year-old mechanic who recorded the protest in support of police on his phone, said the conflict had been stoked by politicians on both sides.
“It’s becoming more and more tense,” he said.
Oriol Junqueras, one of nine Catalan leaders in pretrial detention since the end of last year, announced that he would participate in next year’s European Parliament elections.
“Being a candidate for the European elections is the bestlest way to denounce regression in the democratic values and repression that we have seen from the Spanish government,” he said.
Londonderry: Men Arrested for Ramming a House with a Car
Three men, aged 33, 34 and 39, have been arrested after repeatedly ramming a Londonderry house with a car.
The incident occurred on Thursday around 19:30 in Ballynagard Crescent.
Inspector Bob Blemmings said damage was done to the entrances and the building itself.
At some point, it's possible it's that the car with a crossbow may have been fired.
Menga's attack gives Livingston a 1-0 victory against the Rangers
Dolly Menga's first goal for Livingston secured victory
A promoted Livingston impressed the Rangers by getting Steven Gerrard's second loss in 18 Ibrox club coach games.
Dolly Menga's attack made the difference. Gary Holt's took a spot, with Hibernian in second place.
This season, Gerrard’s have not yever achieved any out-of-home wins and next Sunday they face Hearts, leaders of the table with eight points ahead.
Earlier, the Rangers host Rapid Vienna on Thursday in the Europa League.
Meanwhile, Livingston extends their division winning streak to six games. Holt, starting coach, has not yet savoured defeat since replacing Kenny Miler last month.
Livingston lets out opportunities in front of a blunt visitor
Holt’s team should have gone one step ahead long before scoring, causing all sorts of issues to the Rangers for their direct play.
Scott Robinson overtook the defense, but missed his attempt ahead of goal. Then Alan Lithgow got the deflected shot after stepping in to top Craig Halkett's header in front of the goal.
The hosting team consisted of letting the Rangers play in front of them, knowing they could cause problems for the visitors with the ball plays stopped.
That’s how the decisive goal arrived.
The Rangers gave them a free throw and Livingston looked for an inning, with a combined play by Declan Gallagher and Robinson for Menga, who hit the ball and scored from the center of the area.
