They needed to materialize the plays, since they only managed to enter the opposite field once, which is a wake-up call to the Rangers, who are in the middle of the table.
Erdoğan has received an ambiguous reception in Cologne
On Saturday, September 29, there were smiles and a blue sky during the breakfast of the leaders of Turkey and Germany in Berlin.
It is the last day of President Erdogan’s controversial visit to Germany, with the aim of restoring relations between NATO allies.
They have discussed issues such as human rights, freedom of the press and Turkey’s accession to the EU.
Erdogan then headed to Cologne to inaugurate a huge new mosque.
The city hosts the largest population of Turks outside Turkey.
Police alleged security reasons to prevent 25 000 people from meeting in front of the mosque, but many supporters met in the immediate vicinity to see their president.
Hundreds of Erdogan’s opponents were also hearned, much of them Kurds, who condemned both Erdogan’s policies and the German government’s decision to receive him in the country.
These demonstrations reflect how divisive this visitor is, greeted as a hero by some German Turks and considered an autocrat by others.
Deptford traffic accident: cyclist dies after colliding with vehicle
A cyclist has been killed in a car accident in London.
The crash occurred near the junction of Bestwookd Street and Evelyn Street, a busy Deptford road, southeast of the city, around 10:15.
The driver of the car stopped and the cyclist was treated by paramedics, but died there.
The accident comes after another cyclist died after being hit by a vehicle that leaked on Childers Street, nearly two kilometers from Saturday's crash site.
Metropolitan police said officers were working to identify the man and inform their relatives.
Roads have been cut off and buses have been diverted, and drivers have been advised to avoid the area.
Long Lartin Prison: Six Officers Injured After Riots
Six prison officials have been injured in an altercation in a high-security prison for men, according to the prison’s leadership.
Riots began at Long Lartin Prison in Worcestershire around 9:30 on Sunday and are still ongoing.
Specialists from an elite team (called “Tornado”) are in charge of controlling altercations, in which eight interns participate and that affect a pavilion.
Officers received assistance for minior face injuries at the scene.
A spokesman for the prison service comments: “Specialized prison personnel have been deployed to deal with the ongoing Long Lartin prison incident.
Six staff members have received health care for injuries.
In our prisons violence is not tolerated and we make it clear that those responsible will be put in the hands of the police and could spend more time behind bars.”
Long Lartin prison hosts more than 500 inmates, including some of the country’s most dangerous offenders.
In June it was reported that the director of the prison had received hospital medical assistance after an inmate attacked him.
In addition, in October of last year anti-riot agents were asked to go to the prison to face a serious altercation in which the prison staff were attacked with billiard balls.
Hurricane Rosa threatens Phoenix, Las Vegas and Salt Lake City with flash flooding (arid areas could benefit)
It’s unusual for a tropical depression to reach Arizona, but that’s exactly what could happen early next week when Hurricane Rosa’s last snales cross the southwest of the desert, at risk of flash flooding.
The National Weather Service has already issued flash flood alerts for Monday and Tuesday in western Arizona, southern and eastern Nevada and southeastern California and Utah, including the cities of Phoenix, Flagstaff, Las Vegas and Salt Lake City.
Rosa is expected to head straight for Phoenix on Tuesday, leaving rains by the end of Monday.
The Phoenix National Weather Service said in a tweet that only “ten hurricanes had retained their tropical storm or depression state within 321 kilometers of Phoenix since 1950.
In 1967, Hurricane Katrina approached nearly 65 kilometers from the Arizona border.”
The latest models of the National Hurricane Center foresee between 5 and 10 centimeters of torrential rains, which can reach 15 centimeters in isolated areas of the Escarpe Mogollón, in Arizona.
It is likely that between 2 and 5 centimeters of rain falls, with the possibility of reaching 10 in isolated places, in other areas of the southwest desert, including the Rocky Mountains and the Great Basin.
In places that are not at risk of flash floods, Rosa’s rains can be considered a blessing, since the region is affected by the drought.
Although a flood is a very serious issue, it is likely that some of these torrential rains will be beneficial due to the drought conditions that are currently being experienced in the Southwest.
According to the U.S. Drought Monitor, more than 40 percent of Arizona is suffering at least extreme drought, the second-highest category, as reported on weather.com.
First of all, Hurricane Rosa’s itinerary will hit Mexico’s Baja California peninsula.
Rosa, which on Sunday morning will still have a hurricane force with maximum gusts of 136 kilometers per hour, is 619 kilometers south of Punta Eugenia (Mexico) and moves north at 19 kilometers per hour.
The storm is encountering cooler waters in the Pacific, so it is losing steam.
It is expected to stand in Mexico with the intensity of a tropical storm throughout Monday afternoon.
Heavy torrential rains could fall in some parts of Mexico, which are at considerable risk of flooding.
“ Torrential rains of between 7 and 15 centimeters are expected from Baja California to the northwest of Sonora, with the possibility of reaching 25 centimeters,” as communicated on weather.com.
Rosa will head north of Mexico as a tropical storm, before reaching the Arizona border in the early hours of Tuesday morning as a tropical depression. Subsequently, it will move through Arizona to southern Utah late Tuesday night.
“Rosa’s main danger or its remains is heavy torrential rain in Baja California, northwestern Sonora and the southwestern U.S. desert.
These rains are expected to cause dangerous flash flooding and debris flows in the deserts, as well as landslides in mountain areas.
Midsomer Norton Attack: Four Arrests for Assassination
Three teenagers and a 20-year-old have been arrested on suspicion of an assassination attempt after another 16-year-old man had been found with stab wounds in Somerset.
The teenager was found injured in the Excelsior Terrace area of Midsomer Norton around 4:00 on Saturday.
He was taken to a hospital, where he remains “stable.”
A 17-year-old boy, twelved an 18-year-old and one out of 20, were arrested that same night in the Radstock area in Avon, according to Somerset police.
Officers have appealed to anyone who may have recorded what happened with their phone.
Trump says Kavanaugh "suffered the meanness and anger" of the Democratic Party
“Voting for Judge Kavanaugh is voting to reject the ruthless and outraging tactics of the Democratic Party,” Trump said at a rally held in Wheeling, West Virginia.
Trump said Kavanaugh has “suffered the pettiness and anger” of the Democratic Party throughout the appointment process.
Kavanaugh testified before Congress on Thursday, vigorously and emotionally denying Christine Blasey Ford's allegation that she had sexually assaulted her decades ago when they were teenagers.
Ford also testified in the session about this indictment.
On Saturday, the president said the “U.S. saw Kavanaugh’s cunning, quality and bravery.”
“Voting to confirm Judge Kavanaugh is to vote to confirm one of the most successful legalists of our day, a jurist with an admirable record in the service of the public,” he told his West Virginia supporters.
The president made indirect references to Kavanaugh’s candidacy by talking about the importance of Republican participation in midterm elections.
“Five weeks around for one of the most important choices of our lives.
I don’t introduce myself, but it’s a race,” he said.
“That’s why I’m going from one place to another struggling to get great candidates.”
Trump alleged that Democrats are embarking on a “resistance and obstruction” mission.
The first key procedural vote in the Senate over Kavanaugh’s appointment is expected to be held no later than Friday, a senior GOP leadership official told CNN.
Hundreds killed in Indonesia’s earthquake and tsunami
At least 384 people have been killed and many have been swept away by huge waves ravaging the beaches after a strong earthquake and tsunami on Sulawesi’s Indonesian island, authorities said Saturday.
Hundreds of people had gathered to celebrate a festival on Palu’s city beach on Friday as waves of up to six meters reached the coast at dusk, dragging many to death and destroying everything in their path.
The tsunami occurred after a 7.5-magnitude earthquake.
“When it was warned yesterday that a tsunami could occur, people were still on the beach and didn’t flee immediately, so they were victims,” Sutopo Purwo Nugroho, spokesman for Indonesia’s National Agency for Disaster Management (BNPB), told a news conference in Jakarta.
“The tsunami didn’t come alone, it dragged cars, logs, houses, ravaged everything that was on land,” Nugroho said. The tsunami had moved across the open sea at a speed of 800 km/h before reaching the coast.
He also added that some people had climbed trees to escape the tsunami and survived.
Around 16 700 people were evacuated to 24 centers in Palu.
Aerial photographs published by the agency showed the destruction of many buildings and tents, split and collapsed bridges, and a mosque surrounded by water.
Aftershocks continued to hit the coastal city on Saturday.
The series of earthquakes was felt in an area with 2.4 million people.
The Agency for the Evaluation and Application of Technology (BPPT) reported in a statement that the energy released by Friday’s great earthquake was 200 times higher than that of the atomic bomb that fell on Hiroshima in World War II.
He added that the city’s geography, which sits at the end of a long and narrow bay, may have magnified the size of the tsunami.
Nugroho described the damage as “significant” and said thousands of homes, hospitals, shopping malls and hotels had collapsed.
According to him, bodies of some victims were found trapped under the rubble of the crumbling buildings. In addition, 540 people had been injured and 29 were missing.
Nugroho noted that the death toll and damage could be greater along 300 km of coastline north of Palu, an area called Donggala, which is closer to the epicenter of the quake.
Donggala’s communications “were totally paralyzed and there was no information” from Donggala, Nugroho said.
“More than 300 000 people live there,” the Red Cross said in a statement, adding that its staff and volunteers were on their way to the affected areas.
“This is already a tragedy by itself, but it could be much worse,” he said.
On Saturday, the agency received much criticism for failing to report that a tsunami had hit Palu, although those responsible claim that the waves had already reached the coast when the alert was issued.
In a recording captured by an amateur and shared on social media, a man who was on the top floor of a building is heard warning people who were on the street that a tsunami was approaching.
In a matter of minutes, a wall of water hits the coast, taking ahead buildings and cars.
Reuters could not validate the material immediately.
The earthquake and tsunami caused a large blackout that cut off communications around Palu, making it difficult for the authorities to coordinate rescue work.
The army began sending cargo planes with help from Jakarta and other cities, authorities say, but people evacuated still need food and other commodities.
The city's airport has reopened only for relief efforts and will remain closed until October.
President Joko Widodo has a scheduled visit to Paul's evacuation centers on Sunday.
The death toll from the Indonesian tsunami exceeds 800.
It's a very bad thing.
Although Donggala’s World Vision staff have arrived safely in the city of Palu, where employees take refuge under tarpaulins set up in the garden of their offices, they have seen a devastating picture on their path, Mr. Doseba said.
“I was told they had seen a lot of houses destroyed,” he said.
It's a very bad thing.
Even as aid groups set in motion disaster assistance gears, some people lamented that non-displacement of cooperators with extensive experience was not being allowed to move to Palu.
Under the Indonesian regulations, foreign financing, supplies and staffing can only be used if the site concerned declares itself a national disaster zone.
And that hasn't happened yet.
“It’s still a catastrophe at the provincial level,” said Aulia Arrani, a Red Cross spokesman in Indonesia.
“As soon as the government says it considers it a national disaster, we can start providing international aid, but it hasn’t happened yet.”
As the second night fell in Palu after Friday’s earthquake and tsunami, friends and family members of people who were still missing were still hopeful that their loved ones would be the miracles that brought some light to the bleak stories that write the natural catastrophes.
On Saturday, a little boy was removed from a sewer.
On Sunday, rescuers released a woman who had been trapped under the rubble for two days next to her mother’s body.
Gendon Subandono, an instructor of Indonesia’s national paragliding team, had trained two missing paragliders for the Asian Games, which concluded earlier that month in Indonesia.
Other of those who were trapped at the Roa Roa hotel, Mr. Mandagi included, were his students.
“From my expertise in the paragliding field, I carry my own emotional charge,” he said.
Mr. Gendon recalled how, in the hours that followed the release of the news that the Roa Roa hotel had collapsed in the paragliding community, he had sent desperate WhatsApp messages to Palu’s competitors, who were participating in the beach festival.
However, their messages only showed one gray checkmark, not two in blue.
"I think that means the messages weren't delivered," he said.
Thieves take 26 750 USD during an ATM top-up at Newport on the Levee
On Friday morning, thieves robbed a Brink clerk who recharged an ATM in Newport on the Levee, taking 26 750 USD, according to a Newport Police Department press release.
The driver of the vehicle had emptied an ATM located in the leisure center and was preparing to recharge it with more money, Detective Dennis McCarthy wrote in the note.
While he was busy, another man “came running behind Brink’s clerk” and stole a bag of money destined for recharging.
According to the note, there are witnesses who saw several suspects leaving the scene, but the police did not specify the number involved in the incident.
Anyone who has information about their identities should contact Newport Police at 859-292-3680.
Kanye West: Rapper Changes Name to Ye
Rapper Kanye West will start calling himself Ye.
He announced the change on Twitter on Saturday: “The person formerly known as Kanye West.”
It’s been a while since West, 41, is nicknamed Ye, a name he used as a title for his eighth album, released in June.
The change comes ahead of their appearance on Saturday Night Live, where they are expected to present their new album, Yandhi.
It replaces the singer Ariana Grande, who had to cancel her performance for “emotional reasons”, as commented by the creator of the program.
In addition to being an abbreviation for his current professional name, West had previously said that the word had a religious importance to him.
“I think it’s the most widely used word in the Bible, where it means ‘you,’” West said earlier this year as he spoke the album’s title with radio broadcaster Big Boy.
"It's you, it's us, it means us.
He went from being Kanye, which means ‘the one’, to Ye, a reflection of our good, bad and confusing things, everything.
The album is more a reflection of who we are.”
He is one of the many famous rappers who has changed his name.
Sean Combs has been known as Puff Daddy, P. Diddy or Diddy, but this year he announced that he preferred the names Love and Brother Love.
A former West collaborator, JAY-Z, has used his name in uppercase or lowercase, with or without a script.
Mexican AMLO vows not to use the army against the civilian population
Andrés Manuel López Obrador, Mexican president-elect, has vowed never to use the army against the civilian population, now that the 50th anniversary of a bloody retaliation against students in the country is approaching.
On Saturday, López Obrador promised in Plaza Tlatelolco that he would “never use the army to repress the people of Mexico.”
On October 2, 1968, troops fired on a peaceful demonstration in the square, killing 300 people at a time when left-wing student movements were entrenching throughout Latin America.
López Obrador is committed to providing support to young Mexicans by providing monthly subsidies to those who study and opening more free public universities.
He stated that unemployment and a lack of educational opportunities led young people to integrate into criminal gangs.
U.S
Now that China is more active in artificial intelligence, the U.S. should double its research investment in this field, says Kai-Fu Lee, an investor and professional at I.A. who has worked for Google, Microsoft and Apple.
The comments then arise the announcements relating to the I.A. from various parts of the U.S. government, even when, in general, the U.S
Meanwhile, China unveiled its plan last year: its goal is to become No. 1 in innovation in A. by 2030.
“Duplicating the budget for the I.A. research would be a good start, given that all the other countries go far behind the U.S. We have an eye on the next breakthrough in the field of I.A,” Lee says.
Double funding means twice the chance that the next big achievement in I.A. will be reached in the U.S., as Lee stated in an interview with CNBC this week.
Lee, whose book “AI Superpowers: China, Silicon Valley and the New World Order” was published this month by Houghton Mifflin Harcourt, is the CEO of Sinovation Ventures, which he has invested in one of China’s leading I.A. companies, Face++.
In the 80’s, at Carnegie Mellon University, he worked on an I.A. system that beaten Othello’s best American player. He was subsequently an executive at Microsoft Research and president of Google’s subsidiary in China.
Lee gave recognition to previous U.S. government technology competitions, such as the DARPA Robotics Challenge, and asked when it would be next, which would identify the next generation of visionaries.
In the U.S., investigators often have to work hard to get government grants, Lee said.
“It’s not China that’s taking academic leaders; it’s the corporations,” Lee said.
In recent years, Facebook, Google, and other tech companies have hired prominent people from universities to work at I.A.
Lee commented that changes in immigration policy could also help the U.S
“I think doctors in I.A. should have permanent residence cards automatically,” he said.
The State Council of China unveiled its Plan for the Development of the Next Generation of Artificial Intelligence in July 2017.
China’s National Natural Sciences Foundation funds people from academic institutions just as the National Foundation for Science and other governmental organizations funds American researchers, but the quality of academic work in China is lower, Lee said.
Earlier this year, the U.S. Department of Defense established a Joint Artificial Intelligence Center aimed at attracting partners in the industrial and academic spheres. Meanwhile, the White House announced the formation of a Select Committee on Artificial Intelligence.
In addition, DARPA this month announced an investment of US$2000 million in an AI Next initiative.
Regarding the National Foundation for Science, it currently invests more than 100 million USD per year in I.A. research.
Meanwhile, U.S. legislation that sought to create a National Security Commission on Artificial Intelligence has carried out no action in months.
The Macedonians decided in a referendum on whether they will change their country’s name
The people of Macedonia decided in a referendum held on Sunday on whether their name will change to “Republic of North Macedonia,” a gesture that would resolve a decades-long conflict with Greece, which had blocked its candidacy for accession to the European Union and NATO.
Greece, which has a province called Macedonia, maintains that the name of its northern neighbor is a claim of its territory and has vetoed its entry into NATO and the EU.
In June, both governments reached an agreement on the proposed new name, but nationalist opponents contend that the change would undermine the ethnic identity of the Slavic population, a majority in Macedonia.
President Gjorge Ivanov has said that he will not vote in the referendum and a boycott campaign has questioned whether participation will reach the minimum 50% required for the referendum to be considered valid.
The ballot of the referendum raised the following question: “Are you in favour of accession to NATO and the EU with the acceptance of the agreement with Greece?”
Supporters of the name change, including Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, allege that it’s a price worth paying for Macedonia, one of the countries arising from the disintegration of Yugoslavia, to try to access bodies like the EU and NATO.
“Today I have come to vote for the future of the country, for the young people of Macedonia, so that they can live freely under the protection of the European Union, because this represents a safer life for everyone,” said Olivera Georgijevska, 79, in Skopie.
Although it will not be legally binding, quite a few members of Parliament have said that they will respect the outcome of the vote to make it decisive.
Naming requires a two-thirds majority in Parliament.
The State Electoral Commission stated that irregularities had not been reported at one in the afternoon.
However, turnout was only 16%, compared with 34% of the last parliamentary elections held in 2016, when 66% of registered voters cast their ballots.
“I’ve come to vote for my children, our place is in Europe,” said Gjose Tanevski, 62, who was voting in the capital, Skopie.
Zoran Zaev, Prime Minister of Macedonia, his wife Zorica and son Dushko cast their vote in the Macedonian referendum on a renaming of the country that would open ways to join NATO and the European Union in Strumica (Macedonia) on September 30, 2018.
In front of Skopie’s parliament, Vladimir Kavardarkov, 54, was setting a small stage and removing chairs in front of the tents planted by those who were going to boycott the referendum.
“We are in favour of getting into NATO and the EU, but we want to do it with our heads held high, not through the back door,” Kavadarkov said.
"We're a poor country, but we have dignity.
If they don’t want us to come in like Macedonia, we can establish relations with other countries, such as China and Russia, and be part of Eurasian integration.”
Prime Minister Zaev says NATO membership would provide much-needed Macedonia investment, whose unemployment rate is more than 20%.
“I believe that the vast majority will be in favour, as more than 80% of citizens are in favour of the EU and NATO,” Zaev said.
He said that a positive outcome would be a “confirmation of our future.”
In a poll published last Monday by the Policy Research Institute it was assured that between 30 and 43% of voters would participate in the referendum, which represents a lower turnout than required.
Another poll conducted by Telma TV of Macedonia revealed that 57% of respondents were planning to vote on Sunday.
Of these, 70% said they would vote in favor.
For the referendum to be considered a success, turnout must be 50 percent plus one vote.
Should it fail, it would be the first serious blow to pro-Western government policy since it came to power in May last year.
Look at this: Sergio Agüero, from Manchester City, dodges the entire defense of Brighton to score a goal
Sergio Agüero and Raheem Sterling scooped Brighton's defense in the 2-0 victory Manchester City scored on Saturday at Etihad Stadium in Manchester (England).
For Agüero, it was ridiculously easy to score a goal in the 65th minute.
The Argentine midfielder received a pass in midfield at the beginning of the play.
He ran between three Brighton defenders before entering the open field.
Once there, he was surrounded by four green T-shirts.
He dodged a defender before overcoming several more on the edge of the Brighton area.
He made a pass on the left, where Sterling was.
The English striker used his first touch in the area to return the ball to Agüero, who hit him with his right foot to avoid goalkeeper Mathew Ryan with a shot that reached the right side of the net.
“Agüero has some problems in his foot,” Pep Guardiola, City’s coach, told reporters.
“We’re talking I’d play between 55 and 60 minutes.
That's what happened.
We were lucky that he scored a goal at the time.”
However, it was Sterling who gave the Sky Blues the initial lead in the Premier League contest.
The goal came in the 29th minute.
On that play, Agüero received the ball deep into Brighton territory.
He made a nice ball pass to the left flank for Leroy Sane.
Sane gave him a few touches before directing Sterling toward the second stick.
Sky blues striker threw the ball into the net just before getting out of limits.
City clash with Hoffenheim in a Champions League group game on Tuesday at 12:55 at Rhein-Neckar-Arena in Sinsheim (Germany).
Scherzer wants to water the Rockies
With the Nationals knocked out from the playoff race, there was no reason to force another opening.
Even so, the ever-competitive Scherzer hopes to step on the mound Sunday against the Colorado Rockies, but only if they triumphed against Los Angeles Dodgers in the NL West, they still have playoff opportunities.
The Rockies secured at least one wildcard spot thanks to a 5-2 win over the Nationals on Friday night, but they are still chasing their first division title.
“Although we don’t play anything, at least we can step on the rubber with the certainty that the Denver audience will be encouraged and that the other team would probably play at the highest level I’ve faced this year.
“Why wouldn’t I want to participate?”
The Nationals have yet to announce a starter on Sunday, but it seems they are inclined to let Scherzer throw in that situation.
Scherzer, who would make his 34th opening, did a practice session on Thursday and will return to his normal break on Sunday.
Washington's right-hander track record is 18 wins and 7 losses, with an ERA of 2.53 and 300 strikeouts with 2/3 in 220 innings.
Trump gives a rally in West Virginia
The president made indirect references to the situation around Brett Kavanaugh, his selection for the Supreme Court, when talking about the importance of Republican participation in mid-term elections.
“Everything we’ve done is at stake in November.
It's five weeks before one of the most important choices of our lives.
It’s one of the most important. I don’t introduce myself, but it’s a race, that’s why I’m going from one place to another struggling to get great candidates,” he said.
“We’ve got this terrible radical group of Democrats, it’s happening right now.
And they are determined to regain power by all necessary means, you see pettiness, evil.
They don’t care who they’re hurting or who they have to go over to get power and control, that’s what they want, power and control. And we’re not going to give it.”
Democrats, he said, are embarking on a “resistance and obstruction” mission.
“We’ve seen it these past four days,” he said, saying Democrats “were furious, they were bad, horrible and false.”
He referred to the Senate Judiciary Committee naming Democrat Senator Dianne Feinstein, who received boos from the public.
“Remember his response?
Are you filtering the document?
Mmm, Mmm, what?
No, no mmm, I expect an answer. His body language was terrible, the worst I've ever seen."
The Labour Party already covers a wide range of views.
He's intolerant of people who say what they think
When the Momentum activists of my local party voted to censor me, I was barely surprised.
After all, I’m the last of a row of Members of the Parliament of the Labour Party who is said not to be welcome, and just to say what we think.
My colleague in Parliament, Joan Ryan, received similar treatment to be categorically against anti-Semitism.
In my case, the no-confidence motion criticized me for not agreeing with Jeremy Corbyn.
He disagreed with the importance of responsible economic policy, on national security, on Europe. It is ironic that they were issues in which Jeremy disagreed with previous leaders.
As stated in noticing Friday’s Nottingham East Labour Party meeting, “we want meetings to be inclusive and productive.”
For most of the eight years I have been a Member of Parliament of the local Labour Party, that’s exactly what have been the General Committee’s Friday night meetings.
Sadly, this is not the current tone of many meetings and the promise of a “kinder and kinder” policy is more than forgotten, if it was really applied at some point.
It is becoming increasingly clear that disparaging views are not tolerated in the Labour Party and that every opinion is judged on whether party leaders consider it acceptable or not.
This began shortly after Jeremy became a leader, when colleagues with whom he previously thought he shared a similar political view began to expect him to radically change his mind and to adopt positions with which he would never have agreed, either on national security or on the EU single market.
Whenever I speak in public, regardless of what he says, there is a diatribe of insults on social networks demanding my withdrawal, denouncing the policies of the center, telling me that I should not be in the Labour Party.
I'm not the only one who's experienced it.
Indeed, I know that I am luckier than some of my colleagues, since comments about me tend to be political.
I am amazed at the professionalism and determination of colleagues facing a torrent of sexist or racist slurs on a daily basis, but they never run away.
One of the most disappointing aspects of this political age is the normalization of insults.
Last week, Jeremy Corbyn claimed that the Labour Party should promote a culture of tolerance.
The reality is that we no longer have that wide range of opinions and, with every “censure” motion or every change in selection rules, the party becomes narrower.
Over the past two years, I’ve received a lot of tips that advised me to keep my head down and not make myself heard so much and that’s how “it’d be okay.”
But I didn’t get into politics to do that.
Since 32 years ago I joined the Labour Party as a high school student, motivated by the negligence of Thatcher’s government who left the classroom of my comprehensive school literally in ruins, I have tried to support the improvement of public services for those who need them most, either as a councilman or as a government minister.
I've never hidden my political ideas, not in the last election either.
It’s impossible for anyone in Nottingham East to be unaware of my political stances or areas where I did not agree with current leaders.
The only thing I would say to those who promoted Friday’s motion is that I don’t understand this desire to waste time and energy with issues about my loyalty to the leader of the Labour Party when the country goes head-on to a Brexit that will affect households, businesses and public services.
The real message is not for Nottingham Momentum, but for the voters, whether they are from the Labour Party or not: I am proud to serve you and I promise that no threat of withdrawal or any political opportunism will prevent me from acting according to what I think is best for all of you.
Chris Leslie is a Member of Parliament by Nottingham East
Ayr 38 - Melrose 17: an undefeated Ayr rises to the top
Two rehearsals at the end of the match may have decided to some extent the final result, but, without a doubt, Ayr deserved the victory in this entertaining Tennent’s Premiership day match.
Now they lead the table and it is the only undefeated team of ten participating.
In the end, it was the superiority of his defense, as well as his ability to take better advantage of the opportunities, which gave the victory to the local team, so coach Peter Murchie had every right to be happy.
“We’ve been tested in every game and we’re still undefeated, so I have to rejoice,” he said.
Robyn Christie of Melrose commented: “Ayr deserves recognition, they seized the opportunities better than us.”
Grant Anderson's rehearsal in the 14th minute, converted by Frazier Climo, placed the Ayr ahead; but a yellow card to Scottish captain Rory Hughes, the Warriors' loan for the game, allowed Melrose to take advantage of this disadvantage of the opponent and Jason Baggot achieved an unconverted rehearsal.
Climo extended Ayr’s advantage with a penalty. Before, right at halftime, he scored and converted a solo rehearsal, which left the score at 17-5 in favor of Ayr at the break.
However, Melrose started the second part well and Patrick Anderson’s rehearsal, converted by Baggot, reduced this difference to five points.
Then there was a long interruption due to a serious injury of Ruaridh Knott, whom they had to take out on a stretcher, and, upon resumption of the match, the Ayr went ahead with a rehearsal of Stafford McDowall converted by Climo.
The yellow card to Blair Macpherson, captain of the Ayr, allowed the Melrose to take advantage again that they had another man with an unconverted rehearsal of Bruce Colvine, at the end of a period of enormous pressure.
However, the locals recovered and, when Struan Hutchinson was showned yellow card for grazing Climo without the ball, from the penalty line, MacPherson made a rehearsal when he was in the back of the advancing maul.
Climo converted it, just as he had been doing almost since the game resumed, after Kyle Rowe received the ball sent by David Armstrong from a kick and sent wing Gregor Henry to the bottom to take the fifth rehearsal of the locals.
The protagonist of Still Game wants to orient his career towards the catering sector
Ford Kieran, protagonist of Still Game, seems determined to move into the restaurant sector after it is revealed that he has been appointed director of a restaurant chain.
The 56-year-old actor is Jack Jarvis on the famous BBC show, who writes and co-stars with his longtime comedy partner Greg Hemphill.
The duo have announced that the 9th season will be the last of the series, and it looks like Kiernan is making plans for his life after Craiglang.
According to the official record, he is the director of Adriftmorn Limited.
The actor declined to comment on the matter, although a Scottish Sun source pointed out that Kiernan wanted to get involved in Glasgow’s “blourishing restaurant market.”
“The sea belongs to us”: a landlocked Bolivia is hopeful that the court will reopen the route to the Pacific
Sailors guard a naval headquarters in La Paz.
Navy blandes fly in public buildings.
From Lake Titicaca to the Amazon, naval bases show the motto: “The sea belongs to us by right,
Retrieving it is a duty.”
Throughout Bolivia, a land without sea, they still vividly remember the coast lost to Chile in a bloody conflict over the resources that took place in the 19th century, in addition to the yearning to navigate the Pacific Ocean again.
These hopes may be the greatest in decades as Bolivia awaits a resolution of the International Court of Justice for October 1, after five years of deliberations.
“Bolivia has momentum, a spirit of unity and serenity and, of course, she is waiting for the result with a positive vision,” said Roberto Calzadilla, a Bolivian diplomat.
Many Bolivians will observe the ruling of the International Court of Justice on large screens scattered throughout the country, in the hope that the court in The Hague will rule in favor of Bolivia’s claim that, after decades of intermittent talks, Chile has an obligation to negotiate to grant Bolivia a sovereign exit to the sea.
Evo Morales, Bolivia’s charismatic indigenous president, waging a controversial re-election battle next year, also has a lot to say about Monday’s resolution.
“We’re very close to returning to the Pacific Ocean,” he promised in late August.
However, some analysts believe that it is unlikely that the court will decide in favor of Bolivia and that the situation would change little if it did.
The U.N. body based in the Netherlands has no power to adjudicate Chilean territory and has stipulated that it will not determine the outcome of possible talks.
The fact that the resolution of the International Court of Justice comes only six months after the closing arguments indicates that the case “was not complicated,” said Paz Zárate, a Chilean expert in international law.
Far from promoting the cause of Bolivia, the last four years may have hindered it.
“The problem of access to the sea has been hijacked by the current Bolivian administration,” Zárate said.
In addition, he suggested that Morales’ belligerent rhetoric has undermined any residual Chilean goodwill.
Bolivia and Chile will continue to speak at some point, but it will be extremely difficult to hold talks after this.
The two countries haven’t exchanged ambassadors since 1962.
Former President Eduardo Rodríguez Veltzé, representative of Bolivia in The Hague, rejected the idea that the court’s decision-making was unusually swift.
On Monday Bolivia will be offered “an extraordinary opportunity to open a new era of relations with Chile” and the possibility of “ending 139 years of disagreements with mutual benefits,” he said.
Likewise, Calzadilla denied that Morales, who is still one of the most popular presidents in Latin America, used the maritime issue as a political crutch.
“Bolivia will never give up its right to have access to the Pacific Ocean,” he added.
“Resolution is an opportunity to see that we have to leave the past behind.”
North Korea says there won't be nuclear disarmament unless it can trust the U.S.
Ri Yong Ho, North Korea’s foreign minister, says his nation will never be the first to carry out nuclear disarmament if he can’t trust Washington.
Ri spoke to the United Nations General Assembly on Saturday.
He urged the U.S. to deliver on promises made during a summit held in Singapore among rival leaders.
His comments come as U.S. Secretary of State Mike Pompeo appears poised to resume nuclear diplomatic talks paralyzed more than three months after this summit with North Korea’s leading Kim Jong U.
Ri points out that the continuation of continued sanctions and U.S. opposition to a statement ending the Korean War to subdue the north is a “chimera.”
Washington is wary of accepting the statement without Pyongyang making a significant disarmament movement earlier.
Kim and U.S. President Donald Trump want to hold another summit.
However, widespread skepticism about Pyongyang’s seriousness of giving up an arsenal that the country probably sees as the only way to ensure its security.
Pompeo plans to visit Pyongyang next month to prepare another summit among the leaders.
Paris fashion shows unveil the latest line of huge hats that are about to land on a shopping street near you
If you want to increase your hat collection or totally protect yourself from the sun, look no further.
Valentino and Thom Browne’s designers presented an eccentrical set of oversized hats for their spring summer 2019 collection on the catwalk, dazzling the style of Paris Fashion Week.
Impossible hats have flooded Instagram this summer and these designers have taken their spectacular creations to the catwalk.
Valentino’s most outstanding piece was a beige hat with a wide brim adorned with feathers that wrapped the heads of the models.
Other oversized accessories were watermelons with jewelry, a witch hat and even a pineapple, but they are not designed to keep your head warm.
Thom Browne also unveiled a selection of quirky masks, just in time for Halloween.
Many of the colorful masks had sewn lips and remembered Hannibal Lecter more than haute couture.
One creation was reminiscent of a complete diving team, snorkeler and glasses included, while another looked like a melting ice cream cone.
If you like oversized fashion, you’re in luck.
Stylish hunters predict that huge hats could begin to be seen on shopping streets.
Large hats come stepping hard from “La Bomba”, with a straw hat with a wing of more than 60 centimeters worn by all, from Rihanna to Emily Ratajkowski.
The cult brand behind this impractical hat that flooded social networks presented another great creation on the catwalk: a straw beach bag as large as the swimsuit model who wore it.
The dark orange raffia bag, decorated with raffia fringes and a white-skinned handle, was the main piece of Jacquemus’ La Riviera collection for the spring-summer 2019 season at Paris Fashion Week.
As celebrity stylist Luke Armitage told FEMAIL: “Next summer, I look forward to seeing huge hats and beach bags on shopping streets. Since the designer has had a huge impact, it would be difficult to ignore the demand for oversized accessories.”
John Edward: Language skills are fundamental to the citizens of the world
Scotland’s independent educational centres have a trajectory of academic excellence that has continued in 2018 with another set of extraordinary results in exams that is reinforced by individual and collective successes harvested in the fields of sport, art, music and other community projects.
With more than 30 000 pupils across Scotland, these centres represented by the Scottish Council of Independent Colleges (SCIS) strive to deliver the best level of service to pupils and parents.
Independent schools aim to prepare their pupils to continue their higher education studies, for their chosen careers and for the place they occupy as citizens of the world.
As an educational sector that can design and implement an adapted curriculum, it is observed that modern languages are still a popular and desired subject in centers.
As Nelson Mandela said, “If you speak to a man in a language he understands, the message reaches his head.
“If you speak to him in his tongue, it comes to his heart.”
This is a powerful reminder that, if you want to build relationships and build trust with people from other countries, you can’t rely solely on English.
If we take into account the recent results of this year’s exams, we can see that languages are at the top of the ranking tables, with the highest approval rates being in independent schools.
In total, 68% of students who studied foreign languages earned an A, the highest grade.
The data, collected from the 74 centers that are part of the SCIS, showed that 72% of the students obtained an A in Mandarin, while 72% of those who studied German, 69% of those who studied French and 63% of those who studied Spanish also obtained an A.
This demonstrates that independent schools in Scotland support foreign languages because they consider them a fundamental competence that both children and young people will need in the future without a doubt.
Currently, it is considered that, as a subject, languages are at the same level as STIM subjects (science, technology, engineering and mathematics) in the curricula of independent schools and everywhere.
A survey carried out in 2014 by the UK Commission for Employment and Competencies revealed that, from the reasons given by employers on the difficulties they encountered in filling jobs, 17 % were attributed to the scarcity of language skills.
Linguistic competences are therefore becoming increasingly indispensable for preparing young people for their future careers.
Potential job opportunities that require languages are increasing, so these competencies are essential in a globalized world.
Regardless of the career that someone chooses, if they have learned a second language, they will have a real advantage in the future thanks to a competition like this, which will last a lifetime.
Being able to communicate directly with people from foreign countries will allow that person to automatically get ahead of the competition.
According to a survey conducted by YouGov among more than 4000 adults in the United Kingdom in 2013, 75% of them did not speak any foreign language well enough to have a conversation. It was also established that the only language spoken by a double-digit percentage, 15 %, was French.
For this reason, investing in language teaching is now important for children today.
If they know several languages, especially those in developing economies, children will be equipped for better opportunities to find quality employment.
In Scotland, each school will teach different languages.
Some centres will focus on the more classic modern languages, while others will teach the languages considered most important to the United Kingdom by 2020, such as Mandarin or Japanese.
Whatever the interests of a child, in independent schools you can always choose between several languages taught by teaching staff specialized in this field.
Scotland’s independent schools devote all their efforts to providing a learning environment that prepares children and equips them with the skills necessary to succeed, whatever the future holds.
At the moment, it is undeniable that, in a global business environment, languages still have a vital importance for the future of the country, so this must be reflected in education.
Indeed, modern languages should be considered “competencies for international communication”.
Independent schools will continue to offer these options, diversity and excellence to young people in Scotland.
Il faut well faide.
John Edward is the director of the Scottish Council of Independent Colleges
LeBron debutes with Lakers in San Diego on Sunday
The expectation of fans waiting to see LeBron James debut in his first game with the Los Angeles Lakers is about to end.
Lakers coach Luke Walton has announced that James will play the pre-season opening game to be held Sunday against the Denver Nuggets in San Diego.
However, it remains to be determined how many minutes he will play.
“It’ll be more than one and less than 48,” Walton said on the Lakers’ official website.
Lakers reporter Mike Trudell posted in a tweet that James was likely playing a limited few minutes.
After a training session earlier this week, James was asked about his plans for the Lakers' six-game pre-season program.
“At this stage of my career I don’t need pre-season games to prepare,” he said.
Trump's West Virigna Rally, YouTube Channel
President Donald Trump kicks off a series of campaign rallies tonight in Wheeling, West Virginia.
It’s the first of Trump’s five rallies scheduled for next week, including stops at venues with supporters like Tennessee and Mississippi.
With his candidate’s confirmation vote to fill the Supreme Court vacancy on hold, Trump intends to gain support for the upcoming mid-term elections, as Republicans risk losing control of Congress when the votes are cast in November.
What time is Trump’s rally in West Virginia tonight and how can you watch online?
Trump’s rally in Wheeling, West Virginia, is scheduled for 19:00 tonight, Saturday, Sept. 29, 2018.
You can watch it online via a live stream on YouTube.
Trump is likely to comment on Supreme Courturie’s appearances this week, Brett Kavanaugh, who were strained because of sexual misconduct accusations and have left an early Senate confirmation vote on hold for at most a week as the FBI investigates them.
However, the main objective of this series of rallies is to help Republicans facing the November elections gain momentum.
Thus, President Trump’s campaign signaled that next week’s five rallies aim to “encourage volunteers and supporters as Republicans try to protect and expand the majorities they retain in the Senate and in the House of Representatives,” according to Reuters.
“Congressional control is so crucial to his agenda that the president will travel to as many states as possible as we enter the campaign,” a Trump campaign spokesman who did not want to give his name told Reuters.
Scheduled at Wheeling’s Wesbanco Arena Stadium, tonight’s rally could attract “Ohio and Pennsylvania, as well as have coverage in Pittsburgh’s media,” as reported by the West Virginia Metro News.
Saturday will be the second time in a month that Trump has visited West Virginia, the state in which he won by more than 40 percentage points in 2016.
Trump is trying to help West Virginia Republican Senate candidate Patrick Morrisey who is lagging behind in the polls.
“It’s not a good sign for Morrisey that the president has to come and give him a push in the polls,” said Simon Haeder, a political scientist at the University of West Virginia, according to Reuters.
Ryder Cup 2018: The U.S. team proves it dares to fight to keep hope alive ahead of Sunday’s individual matches.
After three individual sessions, Saturday afternoon’s foursomes may be what this Ryder Cup needed.
The oscillating pendulum of momentum is a completely invented sporting concept, but players truly believe in it, even more in competitions like these.
So where would you say there's momentum now?
“They took six points ahead of us and now we’ve got four, so I guess that can be considered a small boost,” Jordan Spieth said at the end of the day.
Europe has an advantage, of course: four points ahead with twelve more at stake.
As Spieth points out, Americans feel that they have some wind in favor and plenty of reasons to be encouraged, among other reasons for the couple formed by Spieth and Justin Thomas, who played together all day and got three points out of four each.
Spieth has been lethal from tee to the green and leads by example.
Guttural screams of celebration rose in volume as his round progressed, introducing a vital putt to tie the fourth game when he and Thomas went two down after two holes.
The putt who gave them the match in the 15 was greeted with a similar scream, of those who broadcast that he believes that the U.S. team has not yet been left out.
“What needs to redouble your efforts and focus on the game,” Spieth said.
That's what these players have done.
18 holes to make a mark.
The only players with more points than Spieth and Thomas in the last two days are Francesco Molinari and Tommy Fleetwood, the undisputed story of the Ryder Cup.
Europe’s strange and adorable partner has four of four and nothing can go wrong with them.
“Moliwood” was the only couple who didn’t make a bogey on Saturday afternoon, but also avoided the bogeys on Saturday morning, Friday afternoon and the last nine holes on Friday morning.
That streak and the way their energy flows with the bustling audience make it clear that these are the players to beat on Sunday, and that there won’t be other popular players like Fleetwood and Molinari to seal a possible European victory when the sun goes down at Le Golf National.
Preferably the two at the same time in different holes.
Even so, it's still premature to talk about European glory.
Bubba Watson and Webb Simpson dispatched Sergio Garcia, the four-ball hero of the morning, after pairing with Alex Noren.
A bogey and two doubles in the first nine holes buried the Spaniard and the Swede in a hole from which they failed to get out.
But there won't be any kind of help in getting out of the holes on Sunday.
Fourballs and foursomes are fascinating to see closely because you can appreciate the interactions between couples, the tips they give and those they don’t give, and how a strategy can change in an instant.
Until now, Europe has played better as a team and has a great advantage for the last day, but this foursomes session also showed that the U.S. team dares to fight, something that some, especially the Americans, doubted.
Europe advances 10-6 on Ryder Cup
Europe departs with a good lead on the last day of the Ryder Cup, after finishing the fourballs and foursomes with a 10-6 result over the United States.
The motivated duo made up of Tommy Fleetwood and Francesco Molinari led the contest with two wins over a Tiger Woods that was in distress, getting ahead four points in the Le Golf National standings.
The European Thomas Bjorn, trying to preserve the trophy they lost in Hazeltine two years ago, dominated an American team that made many mistakes during the morning fourballs, which finished 3-1.
U.S
American Jim Furyk needs to score eight points out of Sunday’s 12 single games to retain the trophy.
Fleetwood is the first European rookie to earn four consecutive points. In addition, he and Molinari, who received the nickname “Molliwood” after a sensational weekend, are the second pair to win four points in their first four games in Ryder Cup history.
After defeating Woods and Patrick Reed in the fourballs, their superb union served to beat a discouraged Woods and American rookie Bryson Decahmbeau by a resounding 5-4.
Woods, who hadn’t stood out too much in Saturday’s two games, had occasional moments of brilliance, but now lost 19 of his 29 fourball and foursome games, and seven straight.
Justin Rose, who was resteded for the morning fourballs, became Henrik Stenson's partner in the foursomes, defeating Dustin Johnson and Brooks Koepka, number one and world number three respectively, with a 2-1 result.
Although the day was pleasant and quiet in the southwest of Paris, Europe didn’t have everything on its side.
Three-time winner Jordan Spieth and Justin Thomas served as a reference to the Americans, earning two points on Saturday.
They scored an exciting 2-1 win against Ian Poulter and Spaniard Jon Rahm in the fourballs and then defeated Poulter and Rory McIlroy 4-3 in the foursomes after losing the initial two holes.
In the history of the Ryder Cup, that a team recovered from a difference of four points in the individual matches has only happened on two occasions, although Furyk, as a trophy holder, only needs a draw to preserve it.
Although they’ve been the best seconds for two days, it seems that carrying out a counterattack on Sunday is beyond their means.
North Korea says “there’s no chance” that they’ll disarse unilaterally first if they don’t have confidence
On Saturday, North Korea’s foreign minister informed the United Nations that the continuation of the sanctions deepened his distrust in the United States and that there was no chance that his country would unilaterally renounce nuclear weapons under such circumstances.
Ri Yong Ho told the international body’s annual General Assembly that North Korea had adopted “significant measures of goodwill” over the past year, such as ceasing to conduct nuclear and missile tests, dismantling nuclear-tested facilities and committing to the non-proliferation of nuclear weapons and nuclear technology.
“However, we don’t receive any similar responses from the United States,” he said.
“If we have no confidence in the United States, we cannot have confidence with respect to our national security, and, in those circumstances, there is no chance that we will unilaterally disarm first.”
Although Ri repeated North Korea’s well-known complaints over Washington’s resistance to a “phased” denuclearization formula that rewards the country as it takes gradual steps, its declaration is important, as it simply rejected unilateral denuclearization, as Pyongyang has done in the past.
Ri referred to a joint statement issued by Kim Jong Un and Donald Trump following the first summit between an active U.S. president and a North Korean leader on June 12 in Singapore, when Kim pledged to work for the “denuclearization of the Korean peninsula” and Trump ensured North Korea’s security.
North Korea has pursued an official end to the 1950-53 Korean War, but the United States has said that Pyongyang must give up its nuclear weapons earlier.
Likewise, Washington has resisted calls to ease international sanctions on North Korea.
“The United States has insisted on denuclearization in the first place and increased pressure through sanctions in order to achieve its purpose coercively, including by objecting to a declaration of termination of the war,” Ri said.
“The idea that sanctions can cause us to fall on our knees is a chimera of people who don’t know us.
The problem is that the continuation of the sanctions deepens our mistrust.”
Ri made no mention of the plans for a second summit between Kim and Trump, announced with hype by the U.S. leader before the United Nations earlier this week.
Instead, the minister highlighted three meetings between Kim and Moon Jae, leader of South Korea, over the past five months, adding: “If South Korea had been interested in denuclearization rather than the U.S., the denuclearization of the Korean peninsula would not find itself in such a stagnation.”
Even so, the tone of Ri’s speech was radically different from last year’s speech, when he told the U.N. General Assembly that it was inevitable to steer North Korea’s missiles toward U.S. soil after “evilancy president” Trump, on a suicidal mission, called Kim “rocket man.”
This year at the United Nations, Trump, who last year threatened to “totally destroy” North Korea, praised Kim for his courage in the move towards disarmament, but said much remained to be done and that it was necessary to continue applying sanctions until North Korea denuclearized.
On Wednesday, Trump claimed that there was no set deadline for this: “It may take two years, three years or five months, it doesn’t matter.”
China and Russia allege that the U.N. Security Council should reward Pyongyang for its progress.
However, as U.S. Secretary of State Mike Pompeo briefed the U.N. Security Council on Thursday: “The imposition of Security Council sanctions must continue vigorously and without non-compliance until we realize definitive and fully verified denuclearization.”
The Security Council has unanimously imposed sanctions on North Korea since 2006, in an attempt to cut funding for Pyongyang’s ballistic and nuclear missile programs.
Pompeo chatted with Ri outside the U.N. General Assembly and later commented that he would revisit Pyongyang next month to prepare for a second summit.
Pompeo has already visited North Korea three times this year, but his last trip didn’t go well.
He left Pyongyang in July saying progress had taken place, but a few hours later, North Korea denounced that its “demands were those of a mobster.”
In a meeting with Moon this month, North Korea pledged to dismantle a missile site and nuclear complex if the U.S. implemented the “corresponding measures.”
He said Kim had informed him that the “corresponding measures” he sought were the security assurances Trump had promised in Singapore and steps towards normalizing relations with Washington.
Harvard students take a course on how to get enough rest
Thanks to a new course taught this year at Harvard University, all college students sleep more in an attempt to found the growing sexist culture of drinking caffeine all night.
A professor found that students at the world’s number one university usually have no idea about basic self-care.
Charles Czeisler, a professor of sleep medicine at Harvard Medical School, and a Brigham and Women's Hospital specialist designed the course, it is believed that the first of its kind in the U.S.
His motivation to launch the course arose after giving a talk about how sleep deprivation affects learning.
“At the end of the course, a girl came up and asked me why she just heard about this now, in her senior year.
He said that no one had ever told him about the importance of the dream, which surprised me,” he told The Telegraph.
The course, taught for the first time this year, explains to students the basics of how good sleep habits contribute to academic and athletic performance, in addition to improving overall well-being.
Paul Barreira, a professor of psychiatry at Harvard Medical School and executive director of the university’s health services, said the university decided to introduce the course after discovering that students barely slept during the week.
The one-hour course includes a series of interactive tasks.
In one section, there’s an image of a bedroom, where students click on cups of coffee, curtains, sneakers, and books that inform them of the effects of caffeine and light, as well as the impact of lack of sleep on athletic performance and the importance of establishing a routine to go to sleep.
In another section, participants are informed how long-term sleep deprivation can increase the risk of infarction, embolism, depression and cancer.
Afterwards, a campus icon with interactive icons encourages participants to think about their daily routines.
“We know it won’t change students’ behavior instantly.
However, we believe that they have the right to know, just as anyone has the right to know the health effects of smoking tobacco,” added Professor Czeisler.
He stated that the culture of pride for “going through the evening” still exists and added that the modern technology and increasing pressure to which students are subjected meant that sleep deprivation was a growing problem.
Getting enough sleep and good quality sleep should be “the secret weapon” of students to combat stress, tiredness and anxiety; even to avoid gaining weight, since sleep deprivation causes the brain to generate a sense of starvation, that’s why they want to eat all the time.
Raymond So, a 19-year-old Californian who studies chemical biology and physics, helped Professor Czeisler design the course after attending one of his classes the previous year during his first year at Harvard.
He pointed out that the course had opened his eyes and inspired to extend it to the entire campus.
He hopes that the next step will be to ask all graduate students to complete a similar curriculum before joining the competitive institution.
Professor Czeisler’s recommendation is for students to think about setting an alarm to go to bed and another to wake up, and to be aware of the harmful effects of “blue light” emitted by the screens of electrical appliances and LEDs, which can alter circadian rhythms, so they would end up having trouble falling asleep.
Livingston 1 - Rangers 0: Menga's goal knocks Gerrard's men
The Rangers suffered another episode of depression away from home, when Dolly Menga's attack banished Steven Gerrard's disjointed team to a 1-0 loss at Livingston.
Ibrox’s wanted to get their first win away since February 4-1 at St. Johnstone, but Gary Holt defeated Gerrard for the second time in 18 games as a coach, leaving his team with eight points from the Hearts, runaway leaders of the Ladbrokes Premiership.
Menga’s attack came about minutes before part-time and the Rangers’ uninspired line-up didn’t rise at any time.
Now, the Rangers are down to 6th, Livingston rise to third, behind Hibernian due to the goal difference.
Rangers may encounter more problems, as line judge Calum Spence had to receive medical assistance for a head wound after someone supposedly launched an object from the visiting background.
Gerrard switched to eight of the starters who won the match against Ayr, entering the Scottish League Cup semi-finals.
For his part, Holt took out the 11 Livingston players who started a point at the Hearts last week and would have been happy about the way in which his organized team drowned the opponents at all times.
The Rangers may have had more possession, but Livingston took better advantage of the ball when they had it.
They should have scored two minutes from the start, when Menga made a pass into space for Scott Pittman who passed between the defense, leaving him alone in front of Allan McGregor, but the midfielder sent her outside.
Keaghan Jacobs took out an in-depth foul for Captain Craig Halket, but his defense partner Alan Lithgow was only able to send her out for the long stick.
The Rangers regained control, but there seemed to be more hope than conviction about their game in the final third.
There is no doubt that Alfredo Morelos felt that he had a penalty in the 15 minute of the match, when he and Steven Lawless clashed, but referee Steven Thomson turned a deaf ear to the Colombian’s request.
The Rangers got two goal shots in the first half, but Ibrox's former goalkeeper Liam Kelly barely found himself in difficulty dealing with Lassana Coulibaly's header and Ovie Ejaria's boring throw.
Although the goal scored in the 34th minute by Livingston came against all odds, no one can deny that they deserved it for the dedicated effort.
Again, the Rangers couldn't cope with a deep ball-to-ball play from Jacobs.
Scott Arfield didn’t react when Declan Gallagher made the ball for Scott Robinson, who remained calm and had Menga to score.
At the break, Gerrard replaced Coulibaly with Ryan Kent, which had an almost immediate impact, as the midfielder passed it to Morelos, but the impressive Kelly came out of his line to block him.
Still, the Livingston continued to take visitors exactly to the kind of game it likes, with Lithgow and Halkett clearing one long pass after another.
Holt’s could have taken more advantage in the final stages, but McGregor stood firm to reject Jacobs before Lithgow knocked off a head corner that didn’t come in.
Rangers replacement Glen Middleton claimed a penalty towards the end of the game after a clash with Jacobs, but again, Thomson looked the other way.
Almanac: Inventor of accountant Geiger
And now, a page of the Almanac section of “Sunday Morning”: September 30, 1882, 136 years ago today, and COUNTING... the physical future Johannes Wilhelm “Hans” Geiger was born in Germany.
Geiger developed a method for detecting and measuring radioactivity, an invention that culminated with the device known as Geiger counter.
Accountant Geiger, who has since been a pillar of science, also became a pillar of pop culture, as in the 1950 film “Coronate Bells,” with actors Roy Rogers and Dale Evans in the seemingly incompatible roles of cowboy scientists:
Man: “Which the hell is that?”
Rogers: “It’s a Geiger counter, it’s used to locate radioactive minerals, such as uranium.
By putting on your headphones, you can hear the effect of the waves emitted by the radioactivity of the minerals.”
Evans: "It's like a radio!"
“Hans” Geiger died in 1945, when he was about to turn 63.
But the invention that bears his name is still alive.
A new cancer vaccine can teach the immune system to “see” malignant cells
A new cancer vaccine can teach the immune system to “see” malignant cells and kill them.
As part of treatment, the vaccine teaches the immune system to recognize malignant cells.
The method requires the removal of immune cells to a patient and altering them in the laboratory.
Thus, they can “see” a protein common to many cancers and reinject them.
A vaccine trial is generating promising results in patients with different cancers.
A woman treated with the vaccine, who teaches the immune system to recognize malignant cells, saw her ovarian cancer disappear for more than 18 months.
The method requires the removal of immune cells from a patient and altering them in the lab so they can “see” a protein common to many cancers, called HER2, and then reinjecting the cells.
Professor Jay Berzofsky of the U.S. National Cancer Institute in Bethesda said: “Our results suggest that we have a very promising vaccine.”
HER2 “promotes the growth of different types of cancer,” such as breast, ovary, lung, or colorectal cancer, Professor Berzofsky explained.
A similar approach to taking patient immune cells and “teaching” them to attack cancer cells has worked in the treatment of a type of leukemia.
Kanye West embarked on a pro-Trump tirade with a MAGA hat after his appearance on SNL.
It didn't go well.
Kanye West was booed in the Saturday Night Live studio after an erratic performance in which he praised U.S. President Donald Trump and said he would run for election in 2020.
After the third song of the night, called Ghost Town, during which he used the cap with the message Make America Great Again, he began ranting against the Democrats and reiterating his support for Trump.
“A lot of times I talk to someone white and he asks me how I may like Trump if he’s racist.
Well, if I cared about racism I would have left America a long time ago," he said.
SNL began Matt Damon’s sketch program in which the Hollywood star mocked Brett Kavanaugh’s statement to the Senate Judiciary Committee in connection with allegations of sexual assault by Christine Blasey Ford.
Although it wasn’t broadcast, comedian Chris Rock uploaded West’s anger recording to social media.
It’s unclear if Rock was trying to ridicule West with the publication.
In addition, West told the public that he had had a bad time behind the scenes for wearing that cap.
“They messed with me.
I was told not to come out with my hat on.
They messed with me!
And then they tell me I'm asleep," he said, according to the Washington Examiner.
West continued: “Do you want to see how dwelt I am?” adding that he was “going to put on his super-man cloak, because that meant no one could tell him what he had to do. Do you want the world to move forward?
Tested with love.”
His comments received boos from the public on at least two occasions and SNL members appeared embarrassed, Variety reported. One person told the magazine: “The study was totally silent.”
West had been invited to replace singer Ariana Grande, whose ex-boyfriend, rapper Mac Miller, had died a few days ago.
West baffled many when he went on stage to sing I Love it dressed as a Perrier bottle.
West received the support of the leader of the conservative group TPUSA, Candace Turner, who tweeted: “To one of the bravest spirits: THANK YOU FOR RESISTING AGAINST THE CROWD.”
Karen Hunter, host of a talk show, tweeted that West was just “being himself and that’s absolutely wonderful.”
“But I decide NOT to reward someone (buying their music or their clothes, or supporting their art) who I think is hugging and spreading a dangerous ideology for my community.
He's free.
We too,” he added.
Before the show, the rapper announced on Twitter that he had renamed, saying he was now “the person formerly known as Kanye West.”
He is not the first artist to change his name, following in the footsteps of Diddy, also known as Puff Daddy, Puffy and P Diddy.
His colleague, Snoop Dogg, has also been called Snoop Lion, not forgetting the defunct music legend, Prince, who changed his name to a symbol and then the artist formerly known as Prince.
Charge of attempted stabbing murder at a restaurant in Belfast
A 45-year-old man has been charged with attempted murder after a man was stabbed in an East Belfast restaurant on Friday.
The incident happened in Ballyhackamore, according to police.
The defendant is expected to appear in the Belfast Court of First Instance on Monday.
Prosecution will review the charges.
Kit Harington, star of Game of Thrones, attacks toxic masculinity
Kit Harington is famous for his role as Jon Snow in HBO's violent medieval fantasy series, Game of Thrones.
The 31-year-old actor has attacked the male hero’s stereotype, saying that those on-screen roles often conveyed to young boys who had to be tough to be respected.
In an interview with The Sunday Times Culture, Kit said he believes “something has gone wrong” and questioned how to address the problem of toxic masculinity in the #MeToo era.
Kit, who recently married his co-star in Game of Thrones, Rose Leslie, also 31, admitted that he finds it “quite important” to address this issue.
“Personally, I find it quite important at the moment, where have we been wrong about masculinity?” he said.
“What have we been teaching men while they were growing up, thinking about the problems they see now?”
Kit believes that television may have some responsibility for increasing toxic masculinity due to its masculines.
“Which is innate and what is taught?
What is taught on television, and on the street, that makes young people feel that they have to be this concrete form of man?
I think that's one of the big questions of our time. How do we change it?
Because it’s clear that something has gone wrong in the education of young people.”
In the interview, he also admitted that he wouldn’t participate in any Game of Thrones prequel or sequel when the series ends next summer, stating that he’s “fed up with battlefields and horses.”
Starting in November, Kit will start True West, Sam Shepard’s play that tells the story of a film producer and his brother, who is a thief.
Recently, the actor revealed that he believes that meeting his wife Rose is the best thing he has gotten out of Game of Thrones.
“I met my wife in the series, so in that sense, he’s given me to my future family and my life from now on.”
Rose represented Ygritte, Kit's character Jon Snow's love interest in the Emmy Award-winning fantasy series.
The couple married in June 2018 on an estate of Leslie's family in Scotland.
HIV/AIDS: China reports 14% more new cases
China has announced a 14% increase in the number of citizens living with HIV and AIDS.
There are more than 820 000 people affected in the country, according to health authorities.
In the second quarter of 2018, about 40 000 new cases were reported alone.
The vast majority of these were sexually infected, which represents a change from the past.
HIV traditionally spread rapidly through some parts of China as a result of transfusions of infected blood.
However, the number of people contracting HIV in this way has been virtually reduced, Chinese health authorities said at a congress in Yunnan province.
Every year, the number of people living with HIV and AIDS in China increases by about 100 000 people.
Sexual transmission of HIV is a serious problem in China’s LGTB community.
Homosexuality was decriminalized in China in 1997, but the LGBT community suffers a lot of discrimination.
Due to the country’s conservative values, studies have calculated that between 70% and 90% of men who have sex with other men will marry women.
Many of the transmissions of diseases are for not using adequate protection in these relationships.
Since 2003, China’s government has pledged universal access to anti-HIV medicines as part of an attempt to address the problem.
Maxine Waters denies that a member of her staff leaked data from GOP senators and asserts that these are “dangerous lies” and “conspiracy theories”
Rep. Maxine Waters on Saturday criticized accusations that a staff member had released the personal data of three U.S. GOP senators on Wikipedia pages about lawmakers.
The Los Angeles Democrat said those accusations were driven by commentators and “far-right” websites.
“Lies, lies and more vile lies,” Waters said in a Twitter post.
Allegedly, the published information included addresses and phone numbers from U.S. Senators Lindsey Graham of South Carolina and Mike Lee and Orrin Hatch, both from Utah.
On Thursday, an unfamiliar Capitol person posted the information online during a session of the Senate panel on allegations of sexual misconduct against Supreme Court nominee Brett Kavanaugh.
The leak came sometime after the three senators had interrogated Kavanaugh.
Conservative sites, such as Gateway Pundit and RedState, reported that the IP address that identifies the origin of the publications was associated with the Waters office and made the data of a Waters staff member public, according to the Capitol.
“This unfounded indictment is completely false and an absolute lying,” Waters continued.
“The member of my staff, whose identity, personal data and security have been affected as a result of these fraudulent and false allegations, were not at all responsible for the breach of this information.
This unfounded allegation is completely false and an absolute lying.”
Waters’ statement drew immediate criticism on the Internet, including those of former White House press secretary Ari Fleischer.
“Denies this information with anger,” Fleischer wrote.
“This suggests that not the temperance needed to be a Member of Congress.
When someone is accused of something they didn’t do, they shouldn’t get angry.
You mustn't revolt.
You must not question the motives of the accuser.
It must remain calm and serenity.”
It appears Fleischer wanted to compare Waters’ reaction to Democrats’ criticism of Judge Kavanaugh, whom critics accused of appearing too angry during Thursday’s appearance.
Omar Navarro, a Republican candidate who aims to unseat Waters in the mid-term elections, also voiced his opinion on Twitter.
“If that’s true, it’s very serious,” he tweeted.
In his statement, Waters said his office had alerted “relevant authorities and law enforcement entities of these fraudulent allegations.”
“We will make sure that the authors are discovered,” he continued, “and that they are held legally responsible for all these destructive and dangerous actions for all members of my staff.”
Critique of Johnny English: Back in action. Rowan Atkinson's spy's parody falls short
It has now become a tradition to look for Brexit in any new film with a British approach. It seems that this is what it’s appropriate to do with this new installment of the action-comedy parodic franchise Johnny English, which began in 2003 with Johnny English English and came back to life in 2011 with Johnny English Returns.
Is this ironic autosatire about how clearly crazy we are the nation’s new export opportunity?
In any case, the incompetent Johnny English, with his bruising eyes and his expressionless face, has renewed his license to screw up for the second time. Only his name already gives clues that, more than anything, it is a comic creation designed for non-English-speaking film territories.
Of course, it’s the ridiculous secretive agent who, despite his strange pretensions to squander glamour, he has some Clouseau, a touch of Mr. Bean and a pinch of that guy who played a single note on the subject of Charions of Fire that was played at the opening ceremony of the 2012 London Olympics.
In addition, it was originally based on the mysterious traveling and international man portrayed years ago by Atkinson in Barclaycard’s already forgotten television commercials, sowing chaos behind him.
In this last installment, there are one or two good moments.
I loved seeing Johnny English approaching a helicopter dressed in medieval armor as the propellers hit the helmet.
Atkinson’s gift for physical comedy is obvious, but humor falls quite short and is strangely superfluous, especially now that “serious” movie franchises like 007 and Impossible Mission add comic ingredients smoothly.
Humour seems more aimed at children than adults and, for me, the absurd misadventures of Johnny English are not as ingenious and accurate as Atkinson’s silent jokes in the role of Mr. Bean.
Now, the repeated current argument is that Britain is in serious trouble.
A hacker has infiltrated Britain’s super-secret spy network, revealing the identities of all its active agents in the face of dismaying the on-the-service agent, a regrettably small role for Kevin Eldon.
It’s the straw that fills the glass for the prime minister, a pompous and cornered character who’s watching her political popularity sink: Emma Thompson gives her best of herself in this character who’s almost like Theresa May, but the script doesn’t leave much room to work.
His intelligence advisers inform him that, as all active spies are in danger, he will have to resort to someone who is retired.
That has to be the clumsy Johnny English, who now teaches in a young school, although he teaches unofficial classes on how to be a secret agent: there are some good jokes, since it is an espionage academy similar to rock school.
English is transferred to Whitehall for an emergency meeting and reunites with his long-suffered Bough sidekick, represented again by Ben Miller.
Now, Bough is a man married to a submarine captain, a friendly role for which Vicki Pepperdine is not sufficiently exploited.
Thus, the Batmans and Robin who do things terribly wrong in Her Majesty's Secret Service return to action, with the beautiful fatal woman Ophelia Bulletova, represented by Olga Kurylenko.
Meanwhile, the prime minister is falling into the dangerous clutches of a charismatic billionaire in the tech world who claims she can solve Britain’s computer problems: the sinister Jason Volta, played by Jake Lacy.
English and Bough begin their odyssey of absurd situations: they disguise themselves as waiters, set fire to an elegant French restaurant, unleash the chaos by hiding in the luxurious yacht of Volta; in addition, English becomes pure anarchy by trying to use a virtual reality helmet to familiarize itself with the interior of Volta's house.
Definitely, they were used thoroughly in that last sequence, but, however funny and raucous it may be, the set continues to convey a somewhat childish feeling.
Quite a regular product.
As with Johnny English’s other films, I couldn’t help but wonder if the British film industry couldn’t give Rowan Atkinson a role that does real justice to his talent.
The Labour Party denies that it is drawing up a plan for the British to work four days a week, but charge five
Jeremy Corbyn’s Labor Party is going to think of a radical plan with which the Britons would work four days a week, but they would charge five.
Supposedly, the party wants the owners of the companies to allocate the savings obtained thanks to the revolution in artificial intelligence (AI) to the workers, giving them an additional day off.
Thus, employees would enjoy a three-day weekend for the same salary.
According to the sources, the idea “would fit” into the party’s plans and economic agenda to tilt the country in favor of workers.
This four-day change has received the support of the Union Congress, as it’s a way for workers to take advantage of the changing economy.
Just as a senior Labour Party official told The Sunday Times: An announcement of a revision of the board is expected before the end of the year.
"It won't happen overnight, but the four-day labor week is definitely an issue that the party hopes to incorporate to rebalance the economy in favor of the worker, as well as in the party's overall industrial strategy."
The Labour Party wouldn’t be the first to back this kind of ideas, as the Green Party already promised a four-day workweek in its campaign for the 2017 general election.
However, for the time being, not the entire Labour Party supports this decision.
A Labour spokesman said: "The four-day labor week isn't part of the party's policy and it's not going to be discussed at this time."
Shadow Finance Minister John McDonnell used the Labour Party conference held last week to expose his idea of a socialist revolution in the economy.
Mr. McDonnell said he was willing to regain powers from "anonymous directors" and "taken advantage" by companies providing public services.
The shadow minister’s plans also include the idea that the current shareholders of the water companies do not recover their entire participation, since a Labour government could apply deductions that corresponded to the perceived irregularities.
It also confirmed its intention to include workers on the boards of directors of companies and to create inclusive property funds in which 10% of the shares of private sectoral companies have to be allocated to employees, which would entail annual dividends of up to £500.
Lindsey Graham and John Kennedy speak in "60 Minutes" about the FBI investigation around Kavanaugh and whether he could change their minds
The FBI’s investigation into Judge Brett Kavanaugh’s allegations has postponed at least a week the final vote for confirmation as Supreme Court justice and, in addition, raises the question of whether the completion results could influence any Republican senator who finally decides to withdraw his support.
In an interview on Sunday, "60 Minutes" reporter Scott Pelley asked Republican senators John Kennedy and Lindsey Graham about whether the FBI could bring to light information that would change their minds.
Kennedy was more open than his South Carolina colleague.
"Sure, of course," Kennedy said.
"I was in favor of proceeding with the hearing, I spoke to Judge Kavanaugh.
I called him after all this happened, that the allegation came out and I asked him, ‘Did you do it?’
He was steadfasted, determined, sharp."
Graham’s vote, however, seems immovable.
"I already have a decision made around Brett Kavanaugh and I would ignore the indictment," he said.
“Dr. Ford, I don’t know what happened, but I do know this: Brett denied it bluntly,” Graham said, referring to Christine Blasey Ford.
"And none of the people she names have been able to confirm it.
36 years ago.
It's always the same, I don't see anything new in this case."
What is the "Global Citizen Festival"? Have you managed to reduce poverty?
This Saturday, New York will host the Global Citizen Festival, an annual music festival, where an impressive poster of stars gathers for an equally impressive mission: to end poverty in the world.
This year, in its seventh edition, the Global Citizen Festival will see tens of thousands of people move to Central Park’s Great Lawn to enjoy performances such as Janet Jackson, Cardi B and Shawn Mendes, but also to raise awareness about the event’s real goal: to end extreme poverty by 2030.
The Global Citizen Festival, which began in 2012, is an extension of the Global Poverty Project, an international awareness group that hopes to end poverty by increasing the number of people actively fighting it.
If you want to receive a free ticket for the event (unless you can afford a VIP ticket), concert attendees have had to complete a series of tasks or “actions”, such as volunteers, send emails to a world leader, make a phone call or anything else that serves to raise awareness about their goal of ending poverty.
However, what has the Global Citizen achieved 12 years after reaching its goal?
Is the idea of rewarding with a free concert a genuine way to persuade people to take action or it’s just a form of so-called “clictivism,” which is called the stance we take when we think we’re doing something really useful by signing an online petition or sending a tweet?
Since 2011, Global Citizen claims to have registered more than 19 million “actions” of its supporters in favor of a large number of diverse targets.
Thanks to these actions, they claim to have achieved that world leaders announced commitments and policies worth more than $37, which will have an impact on the lives of more than 2250 million people by 2030.
In early 2018, the group cited 390 engagements and announcements that emerged from its shares, of which at least $10 billion have already been disbursed or raised.
The group estimates that guaranteed funds have directly influenced nearly 649 million people worldwide.
Among the most important commitments is “The Power of Nutrition,” a UK-based association of investors and executors with a common commitment to “helping children grow and develop to their full potential.” To this end, they have set out to provide Rwanda with $35 million to help end the country’s malnutrition after receiving more than 4700 tweets from Global Citizens contributors.
“Thanks to the support of the U.K. government, donors, national governments and collaborators of Global Citizens like you, we can mark a small milestone in the history of the social injustice of malnutrition,” Tracey Ullman, an ambassador for “The Power of Nutrition,” told the crowd during a concert in April 2018 in London.
The group also stated that, having carried out more than 5000 actions to try to improve nutrition in the UK for mothers and children, the government announced the funding of a project, Power of Nutrition, with which 5 million women and children will benefit from nutrition-related interventions.
In response to one of the frequently asked questions on your website about what we believe can be done to remedy extreme poverty,
One Global Citizen contributor responded: "It will be a long and hard road and, on many occasions, we'll stumble and fall.
But, as the pro-human and anti-apartheid rights movements have already achieved, we will achieve our goals, because we are stronger together.
Janet Jackson, the Weeknd, Shawn Mendes, Cardi B and Janelle Monáe are some of the performances that we will have at this year’s New York event, which will be hosted by Deborra-Lee Furness and Hugh Jackman.
