“If necessary,” Washington could turn to its Navy to prevent Russian energy from shaking markets, including that of the Middle East, U.S. Interior Secretary Ryan Zinke said.
Zinke alleged that Russia’s alliance with Syria, particularly where it operates at the behest of the legitimate government, is nothing more than a pretext for expansion into new markets.
"I am convinced that they are in the Middle East to negotiate with energy, just as they do with Eastern Europe, becoming the southernmost supplier in Europe."
In addition, according to official information, there are ways and methods of addressing this issue.
"The United States, with its navy, has the ability to ensure that sea lanes remain open and, if necessary, they could even block them to ensure that energy does not end up in the market," he said.
Zinke addressed the people who attended an event organized by the Consumer Energy Alliance, a non-profit group that defines itself as the “voice of the energy consumer” in the United States.
In it, he compared Washington’s approaches in its negotiations with Russia and Iran, and warned that they are, in fact, the same.
"The economic alternative in Iran and Russia basically consists of harnessing and replacing fuels," he warned, adding that Russia only knows how to do one thing, which is to base its economy on fossil fuels.
These claims are made when the Trump administration has been trying to boost the export of its liquefied natural gas to Europe and relieve Russia, which is the cheapest option for European consumers.
For this, members of the Trump administration, including Donald Trump himself, try to persuade Germany to abandon the "inappropriate" Nord Stream 2 pipeline project, which, according to Trump, will turn Berlin hostage to Moscow.
Moscow has insisted at all times that the Nord Stream 2 gas pipeline project, which reaches a value of $11 billion and is intended to double the current capacity of the pipeline to 110 billion cubic meters, is a purely economic project.
The Kremlin defends that Washington’s fervent opposition to the project responds only to economic motives and is another sign of unfair competition.
"I think we all share the idea that energy can't become a pressure tool and that consumers should be able to elect their suppliers," Russian Energy Minister Aleksandr Novak said after holding a meeting with Rick Perry, the U.S. Energy Secretary, last September in Moscow.
The U.S. stance has, however, been met with the rejection of Germany, which has reaffirmed its commitment to the project.
The organization that leads the industry in Germany, the Federation of German Industries (BDI), has asked the U.S. not to intervene in the EU’s energy policy or bilateral agreements between Berlin and Moscow.
"It's a big problem when a third state interferes with our energy supply," said Dieter Kempf, director of the Federation of German Industries (BDI) following a recent meeting between German Chancellor Angela Merkel and Russian President Vladimir Putin.
Elib Elizabeth Warren will look closely at the possibility of running for president in 2020, says the Massachusetts senator
Senator-in-Mass. Elizabeth Warren said on Saturday she will consider joining the presidential race after the mid-term elections.
During an appearance at Holyoke City Hall, Massachusetts, Warren confirmed that this challenge was taking place.
"It's time for women to come to Washington to amend our fractured government so that, finally, there are women among the leaders," she said, according to The Hill.
"After November 6, I will seriously raise the possibility of running for president."
Warren gave his opinion on President Donald Trump during an appearance at the City Council and stated that "he was leading the country down the wrong path."
"I'm really concerned about the damage Donald Trump is doing to our democracy," he said.
Warren spoke uncounteredly about Trump and his Supreme Court nominee Brett Kavanaugh.
In a tweet last Friday, Warren stated that "it is absolutely necessary for the FBI to investigate what happened before the vote."
On Thursday, a poll was published in which, however, a majority of Warren’s own voters don’t think he should run for the 2020 election.
58% of “possible” Massachusetts voters said the senator should not be introduced, according to the Suffolk University Political Research Center/Boston Globe poll.
32% of respondents did, however, support their candidacy.
The poll expressed greater support for the previous governor, Deval Patrick, with 38% support for a possible candidacy and 48% against.
Other well-known members of the Democratic Party spoke about former Vice President Joe Biden and Vermont Senator Bernie Sanders’ possible 2020 candidacy.
Biden said he would officially decide in January, the Associated Press reported.
