18 States, D.C. Support Challenge for New Asylum Policy
18 states and the District of Columbia support a judicial recourse to new U.S. policy that denies asylum to victims fleeing domestic or gang violence.
Representatives from all 18 states and the district filed a "friendly" brief Friday in Washington to show their support for an asylum seeker facing the current directive, NBC News reported.
The plaintiff's full name has not been revealed in the "Grace v. Sessions" case filed in August by the U.S. Civil Liberties Union (ACLU) against federal policy.
She said her partner "and her children, who are members of a violent gang," abused her, but U.S. officials denied her asylum claim on July 20.
She was arrested in Texas.
Lawyers representing these states defending Grace described El Salvador, Honduras and Guatemala, which generate a large number of U.S. asylum claims, such as nations facing widespread issues related to domestic and gang violence.
U.S
District of Columbia Advocate General Karl Racine made a statement on Friday in which he stated that the new policy "ignores decades of international, federal and state law."
“According to federal law, all applications for asylum must be failed by virtue of the particular facts and circumstances of the lawsuit and, instead, this exception violates that principle,” the amicus curiae said.
Likewise, the lawyers indicated in the letter that the policy that denies entry to immigrants harms the U.S. economy by stating that they usually become entrepreneurs more frequently and "suppose a necessary workforce."
Attorney General Jeff Sessions ordered immigration judges not to grant asylum to victims fleeing domestic abuse and gang violence in June.
"Persons who leave their countries of origin may apply for asylum on the grounds of persecution or threats by race, religion, nationality or membership of a social group or a particular political opinion," Sessions said when he announced this directive on June 11.
Asylum was never created as a measure to alleviate all the problems, not even all the serious problems faced daily by people around the world.
Desperate rescue efforts in Palu as death toll increases in race to find survivors
For the survivors, the situation was becoming increasingly dire.
“The situation is very tense,” Risa Kusuma, a 35-year-old mother, told us while trying to comfort her baby with a fever in an evacuation center in the devastated city of Palu.
"Every minute ambulances arrive with more bodies.
And clean water is scarce."
Some residents have returned to their homes destroyed to dig through the few denied belongings they have left in an effort to recover whatever they can.
There are hundreds of people injured and hospitals, also harmed by the 7.5-magnitude quake, are overwhelmed.
Some of the wounded, including Dwi Haris, who has a broken back and shoulder, rested on the exteriors of Palu's Army Hospital, where patients were treated outside the building due to continuous aftershocks, also very strong.
He couldn’t help but cry when he told us about the violent earthquake he experienced while he was on the 5th floor of the hotel with his wife and daughter.
“We didn’t have time to get to safety.
I got caught up in the remnants of the crumbling wall, I think," Harris told the Associated Press. He and his family were in town for a wedding.
