U.S. Ambassador Accuses China of Bullying with "Propagandistic Announcements"
A week after an official Chinese newspaper published a four-page advertisement in a U.S. newspaper promoting the mutual benefits of U.S.-China trade, the U.S. ambassador to China accused Beijing of using U.S
Last Wednesday, U.S. President Donald Trump made reference to China Daily’s payment supplement in the Des Moines Register, Iowa’s best-selling newspaper, after accusing China of wanting to meddle in the November 6 U.S. congressional election, something China strongly denies.
Trump’s indictment on Beijing’s attempt to influence the U.S. election marked what U.S. officials signaled Reuters as a new phase in a sleep campaign initiated by Washington to pressure China.
While it’s usual for foreign governments to run ads to promote their business activities, Beijing and Washington are currently locked in a growing trade war that has led them to increase tariff levels on imports by both sides.
China’s countervailing tariffs that applied at the beginning of the trade war were thought to fall on exporters from states like Iowa, which backed Trump’s Republican Party, Chinese and U.S. experts said.
Terry Branstad, the U.S. ambassador to China and Iowa’s former governor, who is one of the leading exporters of agricultural products to China, said Beijing had harmed U.S. workers, farmers and entrepreneurs.
Branstad wrote in an op-ed in Sunday’s Des Moines Register, China “is further intensifying that pressure with advertisement publications in our free press.”
“To try to spread its propaganda, China’s government draws on America’s precious tradition of freedom of expression and free press by attempting to publish a paid ad in the Des Moines Register,” Branstad wrote.
"On the other hand, in the kiosk of the street where we are here in Beijing, you only see a few discrepanting voices and no real reflection of the disparate opinions that people in China may have about the worrying trajectory that their country's economy is taking, since the media are controlled by the strong pressure of the Chinese Communist Party," he wrote.
He added that "one of China's most relevant newspapers eluded the offer to publish" his article, although he did not specify which newspaper it was about.
Analysts say Republicans are distancing themselves from women ahead of the mid-term vote with the Kavanaugh debacle
As there are many renowned Republicans who are closing ranks in favor of Supreme Court candidate Brett Kavanaugh in the face of the various accusations of sexual assault, analysts have warned that there may be a setback, especially by women, in the next votes that are held in the middle of the legislature.
The emotions surrounding this fact have been very intense and there are many Republicans who have said they want to move forward with the vote.
"In these kinds of matters it's not possible to back down," Grant Reeher, a professor of political science at Maxwell School at Syracuse University, told The Hill in an article published Saturday.
Reeher claimed that Senator Jeff Flake’s (R-Arizona) latest attempt to position himself in favor of the FBI investigation will be enough to quell up displeased voters with this issue.
"Women won't forget what happened yesterday; they won't forget it tomorrow or November," Karine Jean-Pierre, a senior adviser and national spokesman for the progressive group MoveOn, told the Washington, D.C.
On Friday morning, protesters who gathered at the gates of the Senate sang "November is coming!" (November will arrive soon!) at the time when Republicans controlling the Judiciary Committee decided to move forward with Kavanaugh's candidacy, despite the testimony of Dr. Christine Blasey Ford.
"The enthusiasm and motivation of the Democrats comes out of all odds," Stu Rothenberg, a non-partisan political analyst, told the news site.
"People say the repercussion is already very relevant and, indeed, that's right.
But it could be even older, especially among indecisive women in the suburbs and younger voters, aged between 18 and 29, who don’t usually vote even if they don’t like the president.”
Even before Ford’s public testimony in which he had his allegations of sexual abuse against the Supreme Court’s nominee in detail, analysts indicated that there could be a setback if Republicans went ahead with confirmation.
“This issue has become a huge mess for the GOP,” Michael Steele, the former chairman of the Republican National Committee, said earlier last week.
“It’s no longer just a matter of the committee’s vote or the final vote, not even if they sit on the bench at Kavanaugh; it’s also about the way Republicans have handled all of this and how they’ve treated it,” Guy Cecil, director of Priorities USA (a group that assists in the election of Democrats).
However, the Americans seem to be divided; they don’t know who they should believe after Ford and Kavanaugh’s testimonies, although there are a few more who side with the latter.
A new YouGov survey indicates that 41% of respondents definitively or likely believe Ford’s testimony, while 35 percent say they definitively or likely believe Kavanaugh.
In addition, 38% said they thought that, in all likelihood or permanently, Kavanaugh has lied during their testimony, while 30% said the same about Ford.
Following Flake’s push, the FBI is currently investigating Ford’s statements, as well as at least those of another whistleblower, Deborah Ramirez, The Guardian reported.
Ford testified under oath before the Senate Judiciary Committee last week and said Kavanaugh raped her while drunk at the age of 17.
Ramirez alleged that the Supreme Court candidate showed him his genitals at a party when he was studying at Yale in the 80’s.
The inventor of the World Wide Web plans to start a new Internet to compete with Google and Facebook
Tim Berners-Lee, the inventor of the World Wide Web, is launching a start-up that tries to rival Facebook, Amazon and Google.
Inrupt is the latest project of the legend of technology. A company based on Berners-Lee’s Solid open-source platform.
Solid allows users to choose where to store their data and which people will have access to what information.
In an exclusive interview with Fast Company, Berners-Lee joked that the intention behind Inrupt is to "dominate the world."
"It has to be now," he said of the startup.
"It's a historical moment."
The app uses Solid’s technology so users can create their own “online personal data storage” or a POD.
It may contain lists, to-do lists, calendar, music library, and other personal and professional tools.
It’s as if Google Drive, Microsoft Outlook, Slack and Spotify are all available in a browser at the same time.
Something that is exclusive in the storage of personal online data is that the user is the one who completely decides who can access what type of information.
The company calls it “personal training through data.”
Inrupt’s idea, according to the company’s CEO, John Bruce, is to incorporate the resources, processes and skills necessary for Solid to reach everyone.
At the moment, the company consists of Berners-Lee, Bruce, a security platform purchased by IBM, some of the staff-included developers who were hired to work on the project and a community of volunteer programmers.
Starting this week, technology developers around the world could create their own decentralized apps with the tools available on the Inrupt website.
Berners-Lee said he and his team aren’t talking to “Facebook and Google about whether or not a radical change is going to be introduced where all of their business models are completely modified overnight.
"We're not asking permission."
In a Medium post Saturday, Berners-Lee wrote about Inrupt that "his mission is to provide commercial energy and an ecosystem to help protect the integrity and quality of the new website created in Solid."
In 1994, Berners-Lee transformed the Internet when he founded the World Wide Web Consortium (W3C) at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology.
In recent months, Berners-Lee has played a very influential role in debating net neutrality.
Despite even launching Inrupt, Berners-Lee will remain the founder and director of the World Wide Web Consortium, the Web Foundation and the Open Data Institute.
"I'm incredibly upbeat about this upcoming era of the web," Berners-Lee added.
Bernard Vann: the clerig who won a Victory Cross in World War I
The only Church of England cleric who won a Victory Cross during World War I as a fighter has been honored in his hometown 100 years later.
Lieutenant Colonel Reverend Bernard Vann obtained this decoration on September 29, 1918 in the attack of Bellenglise and Lehaucourt.
However, a sniper killed him four days later and never knew he had been granted the highest British military honor.
On Saturday, his two grandchildren discovered a memorial stone at a parade in Rushden, Northamptonshire.
One of them, Michael Vann, said it was "of a special symbolism" to discover the stone exactly 100 years after the battle that made his grandpah deserve.
According to the London Gazette, on September 29, 1918, Lieutenant Colonel Vann guided his battalion through the Saint-Quentin Canal "crossing a thick fog and under the intense fire of machine guns and cannons".
Then he rushed to the line of fire and, with "greater bravery", advanced the line before reaching a cannon with one hand and knocking down three of the detachment.
Lieutenant Colonel Vann was killed by a German sniper on October 4, 1918, just a month after the war ended.
Michael Vann, 72, said his grandfather’s actions were “so great that I’ll never be able to reach their height, but they were very sobering.”
He and his brother, Dr. James Vann, also laid out a wreath of flowers after the parade, which featured the band Brentwood Imperial Youth Band.
Michael Vann said that "it was a privilege for him to be part of the parade" adding that "the value of an authentic hero is evident when he is shown such a large endorsement."
MMA fans were up all night for Bellator 206, but only saw Peppa Pig
Imagine that he stays all night awake to see the entire Bellator 206 so that in the end they won’t let him see the main event.
The San Jose poster had 13 fights, six included in the main card, and in the United Kingdom it was going to be broadcast live at night on Channel 5.
At 6 a.m., just as Gegard Mousasi and Rory MacDonald were preparing to clash, U.K. viewers were stunned when the show switched to Peppa Pig.
Some didn’t surprise them after they had to stay up until early in the morning just to watch the fight.
A follower on Twitter described the switch to cartoons as "a heavy joke."
“There’s government regulation that regulates content that’s issued from 6 a.m. and it wasn’t appropriate, so they had to switch to child programming,” Dave Schwartz, Bellator’s senior vice president of marketing and communication, said when asked about the broadcast.
"Peppa the little pig," yes."
Bellator President Scott Coker said they will modify the program to include British viewers in upcoming editions.
"When I think about the broadcast option, I think it can work," Coker said.
"But it's at 6 a.m. on Sunday, so we couldn't do it until Sunday at our time, which would already be Monday for them.
We're looking at how to do it.
Believe me, when the programming was changed, a lot of messages were received and not all were friendly, precisely.
We tried to fix it, because at first we thought it was a technical problem.
But it wasn’t, it was a governmental issue.
I can promise it won't happen next time.
We'll reduce it to five fights instead of six, which is what we've normally done. We're trying to offer more than usual for the fans to be happy and we passed.
It's an unfortunate situation."
Desert Island Discs: Tom Daley felt inferior because of his sexuality
Olympic jumper Tom Daley says he grew up feeling inferior to the rest because of his sexuality, although this was, in turn, what motivated him to become a winner.
At 24, he told us that he didn’t realize it until he came to high school that “not all of them were like me.”
On Radio 4's first Desert Island Discs show, hosted by Lauren Laverne, she said her intention to talk about gay rights was to convey "hope" to other people.
He also told us that becoming a father made him begin to downplay the fact of winning in the Olympics.
The usual presenter of this mythical program, Kirsty Young, will be absent a few months due to illness.
In Laverne's first show, Daley, who showed himself to be an outcast, remarked that he felt "inferior" to the rest during his childhood, because "it wasn't socially accepted that you liked children."
He said: "Until today, that sense of inferiority and being different has been what has really given me the power and strength to succeed."
He wanted to show that he was “something,” he said, so he didn’t disappoint when he finally discovered his sexuality.
The winner of two bronze medals at the Olympics has become an outstanding profile of the LGBT movement and his image has been used at this year’s Commonwealth Games held in Australia to demand that more countries decriminalize homosexuality.
He said he told him because he felt fortunate to live in freedom, without reprisals, and wanted to convey that “hope” to other people.
The three-time world champion stated that it was also a surprise for him to fall in love with a man, American filmmaker Dustin Lance Black, whom he met in 2013.
Daley married last year's Oscar winner, which takes him 20 years, although he specified that the age difference had never been a problem.
"When you've been so young" (he went to his first Olympic Games at the age of 14 and his father died of cancer three years later), it's hard to find someone of the same age who's gone through the same ups and downs as you.
The couple had a son in June whom they named Robert Ray Black-Daley and Daley said their "global perspective" had changed.
“If I had been asked last year, all the answers would have revolved around ‘I have to win a gold medal,’” he said.
"But he knows what, there's more important things than Olympic gold medals.
My Olympic gold medal is undoubtedly Robbie."
Her son is called his father, Robert, who died in 2011 at the age of 40 after being diagnosed with brain cancer.
Daley said his father didn’t accept that he was going to die and, in fact, one of the last things he asked is if they had passes for London 2012, because he wanted to be in the front row.
"I wasn't able to tell him that I wasn't going to be able to be in the front row," he said.
"He had his hand between mine when he stopped breathing and it wasn't until that very moment when he stopped breathing and died that I realized that he wasn't invincible," he said.
The following year, Daley competed in the 2012 Olympics and won the bronze medal.
"At that point I knew that's what I'd dreamed of my whole life: to compete in the pool in front of mine at an Olympic Games, there was nothing better," he said.
This fact was also what inspired him when choosing his first song, Proud de Heather Small, which accompanied him in the preparation phase of the Olympic Games. he was still playing goosebumps.
Desert Island Discs airs on BBC Radio 4 on Sunday at 11:15 BST.
Mickelson, in low form, stayed on the bench in the Ryder Cup on Saturday
Phil Mickelson will set a record Sunday when he plays his 47th Ryder Cup game, but he’ll have to reverse his form if he wants to prevent it from becoming a sad milestone.
Mickelson, who was playing at the event held every two years to score a record for the 12th time, was ousted by Captain Furyk for Saturday's fourballs and foursomes.
Instead of being at the center of the action, as it has so many times done for the United States, the five-time majors tournament champion had to settle for cheering in a few moments and, in others, trying to perfect his game on the pitch and thus be able to rectify what afflicts him.
Never the most direct of the drivers, not even at the peak of his career, the 48-year-old player is not in his best shape for the Le Golf National course, where the long-lasting rough is continually punishable by erroneous punches.
And, if the field is no longer daunting enough, Mickelson in Sunday’s 9th game, faces brilliant British Open champion Francesco Molinari, who has teamed up debutant Tommy Fleetwood to win the four games they’ve played this week.
If the Americans, who were four points below at the beginning of the 12 individual matches, started in an impressive way, Mickelson’s match could have been decisive.
Furyk showed confidence in his partner, he couldn't say much more either.
"He perfectly understood the role he had today, slapped me on the back and, surrounding me with his arm, said he'd be prepared tomorrow," Furyk said.
"He's very self-confidenced.
He's in the Hall of Fame and he's contributed a lot to these teams before and this week.
I probably couldn’t imagine him playing two games.
I thought they'd be more, but that's how it went and that's how we thought it had to be.
He wants to be there, like the rest."
On Sunday, Mickelson will surpass Nick Faldo’s record of more Ryder Cup matches.
It could be the last of a Ryder Cups trajectory that had never so clearly matched the levels of its individual brand.
Mickelson has 18 wins, 20 lost and 7 equal, although Furyk said that his presence contributed many more intangible values to the team.
"It's funny, sarcastic, witty, he likes to make fun of others; it's great to have him on the team," he explained.
"I think the younger players have loved having it as support, also this week; it was fun.
It brings a lot more than just play."
The European captain, Thomas Bjorn, knows that leadership can disappear very soon
Thomas Bjorn, the captain of Europe, knows very well that, although you enter with a substantial advantage in the last days of the Ryder Cup, it can easily become an uncomfortable tournament.
The Dane made his debut in the 1997 match in Valderrama, where one side captained by Seve Ballesteros maintained a five-point advantage over the Americans, but in the end they only managed to end up with the others stepping on their heels by a minimum margin, winning 141⁄2-131⁄2.
"You keep reminding yourself that we did a great tournament in Valderrama; we also did it at Brookline and we lost, we did it in Valderrama and we won, but for very little," said Bjorn, portrayed, after seeing the 2018 generation win 5-3 on Friday and yesterday leading it with a 10-6 at Le Golf National.
History will teach me and everyone that this team is not finished.
You've got to give it all tomorrow.
Get out there and do what you have to do.
This doesn't end until points are on the scoreboard.
We have one goal to achieve, which is to win this trophy, and that's all we have to think about.
I say it all the time, I focus on the 12 players of our team, but we’re very aware of what’s on the other side where they’re, nothing less, than the best players in the world.”
Proud of what his players have done on a very hard golf course, Bjorn added: "I don't want to get ahead.
It's a different challenge tomorrow.
What prevails tomorrow are individual performances and this is different.
It’s great to share what happens to a partner when things go well, but when you’re there alone, you test all your ability as a golfer.
That is the message that must be conveyed to the players, to get the best out of oneself tomorrow.
Now it’s time to set aside your partner, to go and get the best out of yourself.”
Faced with Bjorn, his counterpart Jim Furyk will try to get his players to do better individually than they did as pairs, with exceptions Jordan Spieth and Justinus Thomas adding three points out of four.
Furyk himself has been at both extremes in the final days of those great tournaments and has been part of the team that won at Brookline and then ended up losing when Europe carried out the "Medinah Miracle".
"I remember every word," he told us when we asked how Ben Crenshaw, the captain in 1999, managed to motivate his players on the last day.
“We’ve got 12 super-important games tomorrow, but we have to start as well as we did at Brookline, as you saw in Medinah.
When we all move in one direction, all the pressure falls on those intermediate parties.
We defined our strategy and put them out with the feeling that we were going to try to do something magical tomorrow."
Thomas was tasked with trying to lead the fight and face Rory McIlroy in the main game, with Paul Casey, Justin Rose, Jon Rahm, Tommy Fleightwood and Ian Poulter, the other Europeans in the second half of the list.
"I went with this group of guys in this order because I think they cover the entire course," Bjorn said of his individual selections.
The new German warship, postponed again
The new German Navy frigate should have been commissioned in 2014 to replace old Cold War-era ships, but it won’t be done until at least next year due to flawed systems and the progressive increase in costs, local media reported.
The delivery of the "Rheinland-Pfalz", the ship that heads the new frigates of the Baden-Württemberg class, has now been postponed until the first half of 2019, according to the newspaper Die Zeit a statement from a military spokesman.
The ship should have joined the Navy in 2014, but the problems that arose after the delivery annoyed the conclusion of the ambitious project.
The four Baden-Wuerttemberg-class ships the Navy applied for in 2007 will replace the old Bremen-class frigates.
We know they will include a powerful cannon, an anti-aircraft and anti-ship missile assembly, as well as some invisibility technologies, such as very small radar, infrared and acoustic signals.
Other important features include longer maintenance periods, so they can be deployed for up to two years away from source ports.
However, the continued delays have caused innovator ships (which were supposed to allow Germany to project its power overseas) will already be outdated when they begin to serve, Die Zeit says.
The unlucky frigate F125 made headlines last year, when the German Navy officially refused to put the ship into operation and returned it to Blohm & Voss in Hamburg.
It has been the first time that the Navy has returned a boat to a shipyard after its delivery.
Little was known about the reasons behind the return, but German media cited a number of crucial "software and hardware defects" that left the ship virtually useless if sent to a combat mission.
Software deficiencies were particularly important, as Baden-Wuerttemberg-class ships will be operated by a crew of about 120 sailors: half of the personnel in the former Bremen-class frigates.
Likewise, it was found that the ship is excessively overweight, which reduces its performance and limits the Navy’s ability to add future upgrades.
The "Rheinland-Pfalz" of 7000 tons is believed to be twice as heavy as other ships of the similar class used by the Germans in World War II.
In addition to defective hardware, the price of the entire project, including crew training, has also posed a problem.
It is believed to have reached €3100 million ($3600 million), instead of the initial €2200 million.
The problems attributed to the new frigates are of particular relevance in the light of recent warnings about the reduction of Germany’s naval power.
Earlier this year, Hans-Peter Bartels, head of the defense committee of the German parliament, acknowledged that the Navy is "running out of ships capable of unfolding."
The officer said that the problem has worsened over time, because old ships were discharged, but have not been replaced with others.
He lamented that none of the Baden-Wuerttemberg-class frigates were able to join the Navy.
The National Trust spies the secret life of bats
This new research, which has been carried out in a state of the Scotch Highlands, tries to figure out how bats use the landscape in their search for food.
It is expected that everything that is found will shed more light on the behavior of the only flying mammals and help orient upcoming conservation activities.
The study, carried out by the National Trust for Scotland, will follow common and sopranous bats, as well as Daubenton-riparian bats and long-eared browns from Inverewe Gardens in Wester Ross.
Special recorders will be placed at strategic property locations to track bat activities during the season.
NHS staff and volunteers will also conduct mobile research with portable detectors.
Experts will analyze the sounds that are collected in the recordings to discover the frequency of bat calls and what species they do.
A report and habitat map will then be produced to create a detailed picture of the environment and its behavior.
Rob Dewar, nature conservation advisor for NTS, expects the results to reveal which areas of habitat are most important to bats and how each species uses them.
This information will determine the benefits of habitat management work, such as the creation of meadows and how forests should be maintained for bats and other associated species.
Bat populations in Scotland and the United Kingdom have declined considerably in the last century.
They are under threat because of the construction and development that affects their nests and the loss of their habitat.
Wind turbines and electrical wiring can also pose a risk to them, as can insecticide papers and other chemical treatments of building materials or attacks by domestic cats.
Bats aren't really blind,
but, due to their nocturnal hunting habits, their ears are more useful than their eyes to achieve their goal.
They use a sophisticated ecolocation technique that locates ants and obstacles on their flight route.
The NTS, which is responsible for caring for more than 270 historic buildings, 38 important gardens and 76 000 hectares of land throughout the country, takes the issue of bats very seriously.
It has ten experts trained in the field, who are dedicated to regularly conducting surveys, nest inspections and sometimes rescues.
The organisation has inaugurated even the first and only reserve dedicated to Scottish bats in the state of Threave in Dumfries and Galloway, which is home to eight of Scotland’s ten bat species.
The property’s director, David Thompson, says it’s the ideal territory for them.
"Here in Threave, we have a fantastic area for bats," he said.
“We have ancient buildings, a lot of veteran trees and all the right habitat.
However, there are still many unknown things about bats, so the work we do here and on other properties will help us better understand what they need to develop.”
It emphasizes the importance of checking if there are bats before carrying out maintenance tasks within the properties, since a breeding colony can be unintentionally destroyed and 400 females and young could be killed, which would mean annihilating an entire local population.
Bats are protected and it’s illegal to kill, harass or disturb them, as well as destroy their colonies.
Elisabeth Ferrell, the Scottish officer in charge of the Bat Conservation Trust, has encouraged the public to collaborate.
He said: “We still have a lot to learn about bats and many other species that we don’t know how their populations evolve.”
Ronaldo denies allegations of rape at the same time his lawyers pursue the German magazine
Cristiano Ronaldo has categorized rape allegations as "fake news", and says people "want to make themselves known" at the cost of his name.
His lawyers have been instructed to denounce the German newspaper Der Spiegel, which was where the statements were published.
Portugal and Juventus striker is accused of raping an American woman named Kathryn Mayorga at a Las Vegas hotel in 2009.
He is accused of having been paid $375 000 to conceal the incident, Der Spiegel reported Friday.
With an Instagram Live video that has already amassed 142 million followers a few hours after the news was published, Ronaldo, 33, called the report "fake news".
"No, no, no, no, no.
“What you’ve said today is false,” says the five-time Ballon d’Or winner looking at the camera.
“They just want publicity at the expense of my name.
It's the same usual story.
They want to become famous just by mentioning me, but that's right, I can't help it.
I'm a happy man and everything's fine," the player added, smiling.
Ronaldo’s lawyers are preparing a lawsuit against Der Spiegel over allegations in what they have called “an inadmissible reporting of privacy suspicions,” according to Reuters.
Attorney Christian Schertz said the player will try to get compensation for "moral damage for an amount that corresponds to the severity of the violation, which is probably one of the most serious violations of his personal rights in recent years."
The alleged incident reportedly took place in June 2009 in a suite at the Palms Hotel and Casino in Las Vegas.
After meeting at a nightclub, Ronaldo and Mayorga allegedly went to the player’s room and there he allegedly raped her anally, according to documents recorded in Clark County District Court in Nevada.
Mayorga stated that Ronaldo knelt after the alleged incident and told him that he was "99 percent" a "good boy" and that he had been defrauded by that "one percent" remaining.
The documents state that Ronaldo confirmed that the couple had sex, but that it was consensual.
Mayorga also claims that he went to the police and that they took photographs of the injuries in the hospital, but later agreed to arrive at an out-of-judicial settlement because she had "terror of reprisals" and worried about "being humiliated in public."
The 34-year-old woman says she’s trying to overturn the deal since she’s still traumatized by the events.
When the alleged assault occurred, Ronaldo was about to join Real Madrid from Manchester United and this summer he has switched to Juve, an Italian football great for a €100 million contract.
Brexit: UK would always regret losing automakers
The U.K. "would repent for a lifetime" if it lost its position as a world leader in post-Brexit car manufacturing, Industry Secretary Greg Clark said.
He added that it was "worrying" that Toyota UK communicated to the BBC that, if Britain left the EU without a deal, it would temporarily stop production at its Burnaston factory near Derby.
"We need an agreement," Mr. Clark said.
The Japanese manufacturer stated that the repercussion that customs delays would have in the case of a no-deal Brexit could lead to the loss of jobs.
The Burnaston plant, which makes Toyota’s Auris and Avensis models, produced some 150 000 cars last year, of which 90% were exported to the rest of the European Union.
"My opinion is that, if Britain breaks with the EU at the end of March, there will be production breaks at our factory," said Marvin Cooke, Toyota's managing director at Burnaston.
Other U.K. automakers have expressed their fears about leaving the EU without an agreement, particularly in relation to cross-border trading, including Honda, BMW and Jaguar Land Rover.
BMW, for example, says it will close its Mini plant in Oxford a month after Brexit.
The main concerns referred to by automakers revolve around the problems that could arise on assembly lines in the event of a no-deal Brexit.
Toyota’s production line is based on “just-in-time” or last-minute production mode, where parts arrive every 37 minutes coming from U.K. and EU suppliers to manufacture custom-made cars.
If on March 29, the United Kingdom leaves the European Union without an agreement, there could be an interruption at the border that, according to the sector, could lead to delays and the shortage of parts.
For Toyota it would be impossible to accommodate the amount of material equivalent to more than a day of inventory at its Derbyshire plant, the company said and in such a case production would be stopped.
Mr. Clark said Theresa May's Chequers plan, where future relations with the EU are established, is "precisely calibrated to avoid such checks at border crossings."
"We need an agreement. We want to have the bestlest deal that not only allows us to get out of the step right now at the moment but we have to take advantage of the opportunity," he said on BBC Radio 4.
"The evidence from Toyota, among many other manufacturers, is that we need to be able to continue with what, until now, has been a tremendously successful set of supply chains."
Toyota hasn’t been able to say how long production would stop, but warned that, in the long term, added costs would reduce the plant’s competitiveness and ultimately costs.
Peter Tsouvallaris, who has worked at Burnaston for 24 years and is in charge of the Unite union at the plant, said its members are increasingly worried: "According to my experience, once those jobs are lost, they never recover."
A government spokesman said: "We have put in place a precise and convincing plan in which our future relations with the EU are delimited."
Trump’s meeting with Rosenstein could be postponed again, according to the White House
Donald Trump's high-risk meeting with Deputy Attorney General Rod Rosenstein could "be postponed another week" as the dispute over Supreme Court nominee Brett Kavanaugh continued, the White House said Sunday.
Rosenstein oversees the work of special adviser Robert Mueller, who is investigating interference in Russia’s elections, links between Trump’s and Russia’s aid, and a possible obstruction of justice by the president.
Anticipation over whether Trump decides to fire the deputy attorney general or not and thus compromise Mueller’s independence has fueled Washington’s gossip for months.
Earlier this month, the New York Times reported that Rosenstein recorded the conversations he had with Trump with a microphone he himself was carrying to try to relegate the president through the 25th amendment.
Rosenstein denied that it was true.
However, last Monday he went to the White House, among news where it is claimed that he was about to resign.
Instead, a meeting was announced with Trump (which at the time was at the United Nations in New York) by Thursday.
Trump said he "would not" fire Rosenstein but the meeting had to be postponed to avoid a conflict with the U.S. Senate Judiciary Committee hearing in which Kavanaugh and one of the women who accused him of sexual assault, Dr. Christine Blasey Ford, testified.
On Friday, Trump asked the FBI to investigate Kavanaugh’s accusations for a week, although this would delay delaying the Senate full vote.
Trump’s press secretary, Sarah Sanders, appeared Sunday on Fox News.
When asked about Rosenstein's meeting, he said: "No date has been defined, it could be this week or it could be postponed another, considering everything that's happening with the Supreme Court.
We'll decide on the fly and I'll report it to the press, as I usually do."
Some journalists would refute that claim: Sanders hasn’t held any press conferences in the White House since Sept. 10.
Presenter Chris Wallace asked what the motives were.
Sanders said that the scarcity of press conferences was not due to an aversion to TV journalists acting grandiloquently, although he said: "I didn't disagree with the fact that they brag."
She then suggested that direct contact between Trump and the press will increase.
"The president performs more question-and-answer sessions than any other president before him," he said, while quoting without evidence: "We've contrasted those numbers."
Press conferences will continue to take place, Sanders said, but "if the press has the option of asking the president of the United States directly, I think it's much better than doing them to me.
We tried to do it many times and you’ve seen us do it many times in recent weeks, so this would replace a press conference when you can talk to the president of the United States.”
Trump usually answers questions when he leaves the White House or when he participates in public sessions or press conferences with dignitaries who visit him.
Press conferences with him only occur very rarely.
In fact, this week, in New York, the president showed why this was the case, where he made a casual appearance and, sometimes bizarre, before the journalists who were gathered there.
Secretary of State for Health writes to NHS Scotland EU workers on their fears of Brexit
The Secretary of State for Health has written to the EU staff working at HHS Scotland to express the country’s gratitude and its desire to continue with them after Brexit.
Jeane Freeman MSP sent a letter just six months before the U.K. withdrew from the EU.
The Scottish government has already pledged to cover the cost of citizenship applications for EU members working in public services that do not depend on central power.
In her letter, Ms. Freeman wrote: “During the summer, negotiations between the United Kingdom and the European Union have followed on the exit in which the decisions expected this autumn were taken.
However, the UK government has also intensified preparations around a possible non-dealing scenario.
I know all of this must be very puzzling for you.
For this reason, I would now like to reiterate how much I appreciate the contribution of each of the staff members, regardless of their nationality.
Practitioners from across the EU and other countries bring valuable experience and many skills that reinforce and improve the work of the health service, from which the patients and the communities we serve benefit.
Scotland is, without a doubt, your home and we would love to continue to count on you."
Christion Abercrombie is undergoing emergency surgery after suffering a head injury
Christion Abercrombie, a Tennessee State Tigers player, underwent emergency surgery after suffering a head injury last Saturday when they lost 31-27 to Vanderbilt Commodores, Mike Organ of the Tennesse reported.
Tennessee State coach Rod Reed told reporters that the injury occurred shortly before the break.
"He came to the band and collapsed right there," Reed said.
Both trainers and medical staff administered oxygen to Abercrombie on the sideline before placing him on a stretcher and leading him to be examined thoroughly.
A Tennessee State member told Chris Harris of WSMV in Nashville, Tennessee, that Abercrombie had exited surgery at Vanderbilt Medical Center.
Harris added that "there were no details about the type of injury yet or the damage caused" and the Tennessee State is trying to find out when it happened.
Abercrombie, redshirt sophomore, is playing in its first season with Tennessee State after being traded from Illinois.
He made five tackles on Saturday befor leaving the field, with which he added a total of 18 throughout the season.
Foreigners will be subject to a higher farm transmission tax when they purchase property in the UK
Foreigners will be subject to a higher estate transmission tax when they purchase a property in the UK and the extra money raised with this measure will be used to shelter the “homeless,” according to the Conservatives’ new plans
The change will neutralize Corbyn’s offensive success to attract younger voters
The increase in the transfer tax of farms will be imposed on those people who are not paying taxes in the United Kingdom
The Treasury expects to raise up to £120 million a year that will go to the “homeless”
The additional money that is raised with the increase in the tax of transmission of farms to foreign buyers of properties in the United Kingdom will go to aid to the "homeless", announced today Theresa May.
The move is understood as an attempt to neutralize Jeremy Corbyn’s success with his promises to provide more affordable housing to attract younger voters and harass those with higher incomes.
The increase in the transfer tax of farms will be taxed on people and companies who do not pay taxes in the UK and that money will go towards incentivizing the government’s policy to combat the problem of begging on the streets.
The surcharge, which is a supplement to the current transmission tax, including also the increase in levels that was introduced two years ago on second-hand housing and the "buy-to-let" system, could reach up to 3%.
The Treasury expects to raise up to £120 million a year with this change.
It is estimated that 13% of London’s re-built properties are bought by non-residents in the U.K., leading to rising prices and making it difficult for first-time people to access affordable housing.
Many of the country’s wealthiest areas (especially in the capital) have become “ghost cities” due to the large number of foreign buyers who spend most of their time outside the country.
The new policy comes just weeks after Boris Johnson called for a transfer tax cut in order to help young people buy their first home.
He accused the big builders of maintaining very high property prices by avidly buying unintended land and urged Ms. May to eliminate quotas that apply to affordable homes to solve Britain's "shameful housing problem".
Mr. Corbyn has announced a number of very attractive reforms for housing, which include rental and end-of-eviction checks for no reason.
It also proposes giving local powers more powers to build new housing.
Ms. May said: “Last year I said I would dedicate my position as Prime Minister to restoring the British dream of improving the lives of each new generation.
And that means solving the housing market, which is so devastated.
Britain will always be open to those who want to live, work and build a life here.
However, it cannot be that people who do not live in the United Kingdom, as well as foreign companies, can buy homes more easily than the British working class.
The dream of owning a house has turned into something too hard for so many people and the humiliation of having to sleep on the street is still too real."
Jack Ross: "My primary goal is to direct Scotland"
Sunderland’s coach says his “primary goal” is to become Scotland’s coach at some point.
The 42-year-old is excited about revitalizing the north-east club, which at the moment ranks third in League One, three points from first.
He went to Stadium of Light this summer after taking St. Mirren back to the Scottish Premier the final season.
"I wanted to play for my country when I was a player.
But I only got a B cap," Ross told Sportsound at BBC Scotland.
"But I grew up seeing Scotland in Hampden with my father as a child and it's something he's always made him want to come back.
That opportunity will only come true, however, if I manage a club successfully."
Ross’s predecessors as Sunderland leader include Dick Advocaat, David Moyes, Sam Allardyce, Martin O’Neill, Roy Keane, Gus Poyet and Paulo Di Canio.
Former Alloa Athletic manager says he felt no fear when he went on to such established names in such a big club, after rejecting proposals from Barnsley and Ipswich Town.
“Right now, success for me is measured based on whether I can return this club to the Premier League.
because the structure and facilities of this club undoubtedly correspond to those of the Premier League," he said.
“It’s not easy to achieve it, but I could only consider that I’ve achieved my goal here when I can return the club to where it should be.”
Ross has only been working as a coach for three years, after being an assistant at Dumbarton and a 15-month period in the Hearts coaching staff.
Afterwards, he helped the Alloa recover from relegation to the third division and caused St. Mirren to move from being on the verge of relegation to winning the championship in the following season.
In fact, Ross claims that he feels more comfortable now than ever before during his career as a player in Clyde, Hartlepool, Falkirk, St Mirren and Hamilton Academical.
"It was probably a real crossroad," he recalled in reference when he took over the Alloa.
"I always believed that the direction was what was best for me, much more to play.
It may sound weird, because it was good, I was able to earn my life very well with it and I enjoyed some epic moments.
But playing can be very hard.
You've got to face a lot of things every week.
I still suffer the stress and pressure of that job; however, in the direction I feel good.
I always wanted to train and now I have managed to do it, it is the time of my adult life in which I have felt better, without a doubt."
You can hear the entire interview on Sportsound on Monday, September 30, at Radio Scotland between 12:00 and 13:00 BST
According to a survey, the perfect time for a pint is 5.30 p.m
The summer heatwave has boosted earnings in British pubs struggling to survive, but concentrated all the pressure on restaurant chains.
Groups of pubs and bars saw their sales increase by 2.7% in July, while restaurant earnings fell by 4.8%, the figures revealed.
Peter Martin of the CGA business consultancy, which was in charge of collecting the figures, commented: "The heat of these days and the fact that England continues in the World Cup have still made that in July the pattern of the previous month of June is maintained, in which pubs increased their profits by 2.8%, although restaurants have fallen even more.
The 1.8 percent drop in restaurant commercial activity in June was even worse in July.
Pubs and bars serving mainly drinks had their strongest month, compared to most restaurants, in which they reduced profits.
Pubs that serve mainly meals also suffered with the heat, although not as drastically as restaurant operators.
Looks like people just want to go out to drink.
In managed bars and pubs, beverage sales rose to 6.6% during the month, while meals fell by 3%.
Paul Newman of RSM’s hospitality and leisure analysts said: “These results follow the trend we’ve seen since late April.
The time and impact of very important social or sporting events remain the most relevant factors when it comes to sales in the out-of-the-home activities market.
It’s not surprising that restaurant groups continue to struggle, although the 4.8% drop in sales compared to last year is going to be especially painful in view of continued cost pressures.
The long and hot summer couldn’t have come at a worse time for gastronomic operators and the weather will tell if the more moderate temperatures we’ve had in August will provide the relief they need.
The total increase in sales in pubs and restaurants, including the new ones they have opened, was 2.7% in July, reflecting the slow decline in brand implementations.
Coffer Peach Tracker, which oversees sales in Britain’s restaurants, bars and pubs sector, collects and analyzes performance data from 47 operating groups, with a combined turnover of more than £90 billion and is the market’s reference value.
One in five children have secret social media accounts behind their parents’ backs
One in five children (some even 11 years old) have secret social media accounts behind their parents and teachers, a survey reveals
The survey, conducted at 20 000 high school students, revealed a growth in fake Instagram pages
The news has heightened fears that sexual content will be published
20% of the students said they had a "major" account that shows their parents
One in five children (some even 11) create social media accounts that hide adults.
A survey of 20 000 high school students revealed the rapid growth that has been experienced in fake Instagram accounts, a photo-sharing reference site.
The news has heightened fears that sexual content will be published.
20% of the students said they had a healthy "major account" that they showed their parents, while they had others private.
A mother who happened to find her 13-year-old daughter's secret site saw how a teenager encouraged others to "rape."
The research, conducted by the Digital Awareness UK and the Headmasters" and Headmistres" Conference (HMC) of independent schools, found that 40% of children between the ages of 11 and 18 had two profiles, and half of them admitted to having private accounts.
Mike Buchanan, director of HMC, said: “It’s alarming that so many teens are tempted to create spaces online where their parents and teachers can’t find them.”
Eilidh Doyle will be the "voice of athletes" in Scotland's athletics federation directive
Eilidh Doyle has been chosen to serve on Scotland’s athletics federation directive as non-executive director at the management body’s annual general meeting.
Doyle is Scotland's most award-winning athlete on track and pitch and its president, Ian Beattie, described the change as a great opportunity for those who want the sport to benefit from its great experience internationally over the past decade.
“Eilidh is recognised and respected throughout the athletics community in Scotland, the UK and the entire world, and we are confident that athletics in Scotland will benefit enormously froming it in its leadership,” Beattie said.
Doyle said: “I like to be the athletes’ spokeswoman and I look forward to being able to contribute things and help develop the sport in Scotland.”
The American, who won the 200 meters and 400 meters at the 1996 Summer Olympics in Atlanta among the four golds he won and is now a regular expert on the BBC, suffered a transient ischemic attack that left him unable to walk.
He wrote on Twitter: "A month ago, I suffered a stroke.
I couldn't walk.
According to the doctors, only time would determine if I would recover or to what extent I would.
It’s been exhausting, but I managed to fully recover, I had to learn again to walk and I’m currently doing agility exercises.
Thank you for the messages and words of encouragement!”
The announcement of breastplates comparing mothers with cows divides opinion on the Internet
A sucker company has divided public opinion on the Internet with an aud that compares mothers who breastfeed with cows they milk.
To mark the launch of what in theory is the "world's first silent breast pump", consumer technology company Elvie published an announcement inspired by a somewhat ironic music video showing mothers who tell the freedom given to them by the new milk squeezer.
Four real women dance in a hay-filled barn as a dance floor, along with some cows, with lyrics like: "Yeah, I milk myself, but I don't have a queue" and "Just if you haven't noticed it, they'ren't udder, they're my breasts."
The choir goes on to say, "Keep them up, milk them, I'm feeding their babies, milking them, I'm milking my women."
However, the announcement, which was published on the company’s Facebook page, has created great controversy.
With 77 000 views and hundreds of comments, the video has received reactions from all kinds of people who have seen it, where many say it manages to put the focus on the "horrors" of the dairy sector.
"An unfortunate decision to put cows to announce this product.
“Like us, they have to get pregnant before giving birth in order to produce milk, with the exception that they are taken away from their babies days after giving birth.”
The Elvie breast pump adapts discreetly into a lactation bra (Elvie/Mother).
Another person commented: "Traumatic for the mother and the baby.
But, yes, why not use them to advertise a breast pump for mothers who do stay with their babies?”
Another comment was: “What a more far-fetched ad.”
However, other people defended the ad, as a woman who admitted that the song turned out to be “hillying.”
"I think it's a great idea.
If I'd still suck, I'm sure I'd have one.
Using the bomb, I felt totally like a cow.
The ad is a bit crazy, but I don’t give it any more spins.
It’s a product genius,” one woman told us.
“It’s a funny ad aimed at mothers who use a breastfeeding pump (sometimes in the workplace or in public toilets), which makes them feel like cows.
In the announcement, the dairy sector is neither praised nor judged.”
In the end video, the group of women show that they have been dancing with the discreet bombs placed inside the bras.
The concept of the campaign is based on the perception that many women who take out their milk with the pump say they feel like cows.
However, the Elvie Pump is totally silent, has no wires or tubes, and quietly engages inside the nursing bra, which allows its users to move, fasten their babies and even go outside with the pump running.
Ana Balarin, partner and ECD of Mother, comments: “The Elvie Pump is such a revolutionary product that it deserved a daring and provocative launch.
Showcasing the parallelism between expressive women and dairy cows, what we intended was to draw attention to the use of breastfeeding pumps and their challenges, while demonstrating in an entertaining and close way the incredible sense of freedom offered by the new pump.
This isn’t the first time the Elvie bomb has made headlines.
During the London Fashion Week, a mother of two children paraded down the runway for designer Marta Jakubowski while wearing the product.
Hundreds of immigrant children discreetly displaced to Texas border camp
The number of detained immigrant minors has experienced a considerable increase, even though the monthly influx at the border has virtually remained at the usual levels. In part, this is due to the fact that the harsh rhetoric and policies introduced by the Trump administration are complicating the possibility of sponsoring these children.
Traditionally, the majority of godparents, who were also undocumented immigrants at the time, feared that the fact of proposing to welcome a child would jeopardize their own stay in the country.
This risk increased in June, when federal authorities announced that potential sponsors and other adult members of their families should submit their fingerprints and that the data would be shared with government immigration entities.
Last week, Matthew Albence, a senior U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement official, testified before Congress that the agency had arrested dozens of people who sought to sponsor unaccompanied minors.
The agency later confirmed that 70% of those arrested had no criminal records.
“About 80 percent of sponsors or their families are in the country illegally, and a large of them are criminal immigrants.
Therefore, we continue to persecute these people,” Albence said.
In their efforts to prosecute minors faster, officials have introduced new rules that will require some of them to appear in court within a maximum period of one month of detention, rather than 60 days, which was the previous standard, according to shelter workers.
Many of them will testify by videoconferencing, rather than in person, to defend their legal status before an immigration judge.
Those who do not consider themselves eligible for amparo will be deported as soon as possible.
According to the workers of the shelters and the reports of the system that have emerged in recent months, the longer they remain in custody, the greater the likelihood that they will suffer anxiety or depression, which could lead to violent outbursts or escape attempts.
According to advocates, these concerns increase even more in larger facilities, such as Tornillo, where it is more likely that, due to their size, signs of discomfort in these children will be overlooked.
In addition, moving to the camp without having given them enough time to prepare them emotionally or to say goodbye to their friends could cause them trauma that many are already suffering.
Syria calls on U.S
In a speech to the U.N. General Assembly, Foreign Minister Walid al-Moualem also called on Syrian refugees to return to their homes, even though the country’s war is in its eighth year.
Moualem, who is also the Deputy Prime Minister, said foreign forces were illegally in Syrian territory under the pretext of the fight against terrorism and that the issue “will be dealt with accordingly.”
“They must withdraw immediately and unconditionally,” he told the assembly.
Moualem insisted that the “war against terrorism has practically already ended” in Syria, where more than 360,000 people have died since 2011 and several million have been uprooted from their homes.
He said Damascus would continue to “fight in this sacred battle until we purge all Syrian territories” of both terrorist groups and “any illegal foreign presence.”
The U.S. has about 2,000 troops in Syria, mainly by providing training and advice to Syrian Kurdish and Arab forces that oppose President Bashar al-Assad.
France has more than 1,000 landowners in this war-torn country.
On the issue of refugees, Moualem affirmed that adequate conditions were given for them to return and blamed “some Western countries” for “sowing irrational fears” that made refugees decide to stay out of the country.
“We have asked the international community and humanitarian organizations to facilitate these returns,” he said.
“They’re politicizing what should be a purely humanitarian problem.”
The U.S. and the European Union have already warned that they won’t help with the reconstruction of Syria until there is a political agreement between Assad and the opposition to end the war.
U.N. diplomats say a recent deal between Russia and Turkey to establish a contention zone in Idlib’s last major rebel stronghold has created an opportunity to force political talks.
The Russia-Turkey agreement prevented a large-scale assault by Syrian and Russian forces in the province, where three million people live.
However, Moualem has made it clear that the agreement had “clear deadlines” and has expressed the hope that military action will focus on the jihadists, including Nusra (linked to Al-Qaeda) Front fighters, who “will be eradicated.”
Staffan de Mistura, a U.N. special envoy, hopes to convene soon the first meetings of a new committee composed of members of government and the opposition to draft a post-war constitution for Syria and pave the way for elections.
Moualem set the conditions for the Syrian government’s involvement in the committee, arguing that the panel’s work should be restricted “to revising the articles of the current constitution” and warned against any interference.
Why Trump Will Win a Second Term
According to that logic, Trump would win re-election in 2020, unless, as many liberal viewers, an impeachment or a scandal prematurely end his presidency.
This would undoubtedly be “The most dramatic ending of a presidency ever!”
At the moment there are no signs of fatigue in the spectators.
Since 2014, prime-time ratings have doubled to 1.05 million for CNN and have almost tripled as much as 1.6 million for the MSNBC.
Fox News has an average of 2.4 million viewers in prime time, compared with 1.7 million four years ago, according to Nielsen, and MSNBC’s “The Rachel Maddow Show” has surpassed cable channel rates with 3.5 million viewers for major evening news programs.
“It’s a strange attraction we haven’t yever grasped yet,” says Neal Baer, author-producer of “Designated Survivor,” an ABC drama series about a cabinet secretary who becomes president after an attack destroyed the Capitol.
Nell Scovell, a veteran comedy writer and author of “Just the Funny Parts: And a Few Hard Truths About Sneaking Into the Hollywood Boys’ Club,” has another theory.
He recalls a taxi ride he made in Boston ahead of the 2016 election.
The taxi driver told him he would vote for Trump.
"Why?" he asked.
“'Cause it makes me laugh," was the response Scovell received.
Chaos is always on the rise in the world of entertainment.
Obviously, and unlike anything else seen on television, arguments coming out of Washington could determine the future of the Roe v. Wade case, the possibility that immigrant families can reunite or the health of the global economy.
Disconnecting is a luxury that only the most privileged spectators can afford.
However, the information pushes the boundaries when you realize you’ve been watching a panel of experts six hours debating the use of “deep background” sources that Bob Woodward used for his book “Fear,” Paul Manafort’s 15,000-dollar ostricle leather hunter (“an uncompromising pledge of pride,” in the words of The Washington Post) and the implications of Trump’s rugged descriptions
As far as I'm concerned, I'll never see Super Mario again in the same way.
“By offering something new every night, it’s partly what makes it all look like a reality show,” says Brent Montgomery, CEO of Wheelhouse Entertainment and creator of “Pawn Stars,” when he talks to us about the alternating cast of Trump’s show and his daily plot twists (whether it’s getting into an anti-NFL fight or praising Kim Jong-un).
You can't afford to miss an episode if you don't want to be left behind.
When I went to talk to Mr. Fleiss this week, it was a sunny 27-degree day on the north coast of Kauai, but he was sheltered inside the house watching MSNBC while recording CNN.
He couldn’t get off the TV, not Brett Kavanaugh ready to confront the Senate Judiciary Committee and the future of the Supreme Court hanging in a thread.
“I remember when in those days we did all those crazy programs and people told us that that was the beginning of the end of Western civilization,” Fleiss tells me.
"I thought it was all a joke, but in the end it turned out they were right."
Amy Chozick, writer of The Times for business, politics and media, is the author of the memoir “Chasing Hillary”.
Foreign money flows in the tightestest mid-term elections in the House of Representatives
It’s no surprise the influx of money that’s coming to Pennsylvania’s 17th district, thanks to the restructuring of a congressional district with two candidates seeking re-election for the same seat.
This residential district of Pittsburg, whose structure is recently redesigned, has Democrat Rep. Conor Lamb, who won his seat in another district in a special election last spring.
Lamb faces another re-election candidate, Republican Keith Rothfus, who currently represents Pennsylvania’s former 12th district, a district that largely overlaps with the new 17.
The maps were redesigned after the Pennsylvania Supreme Court ruled in January that the former districts had unconstitutionally been manipulated on behalf of Republicans.
The race for the new 17 has sparked a funding contest for campaigns between the main financial arms of the parties, the National Democratic Campaign Committee (DCCC) and the Republican National Campaign Committee (NRCC).
Lamb’s name became very familiar in Pennsylvania after a close victory in a special election held in March for Pennsylvania’s 18th Congressional District.
That seat had been occupied by a Republican for more than a decade and President Donald Trump won the district by 20 points.
Political experts have given Democrats a slight advantage.
The United States considered penalizing El Salvador for its support for China, although after backing down
The diplomats noted that the Dominican Republic and Panama had already recognized Beijing, with a slight rejection by Washington.
Trump had an affable meeting with President Juan Carlos Varela of Panama in June 2017 and held a hotel in Panama until partners evicted the Trump Organization administration team.
State Department officials decided to return the heads of U.S. diplomatic missions in El Salvador, the Dominican Republic and Panama due to “recent decisions to stop recognizing Taiwan,” Heather Nauert, the department’s spokeswoman, said in a statement this month.
But it was only decided to sanction El Salvador, which had already received aid of about 140 million U.S. dollars 2017 for, among other issues, control of drug trafficking, development and economic support.
The proposed sanctions, which included cuts to financial aid and restrictions on certain visas, would have been very painful for the Central American country and its high rates of unemployment and crime.
As internal gatherings progressed, U.S. and Central American officials postponed a high-level conference focused on security and economic prosperity as a follow-up to a similar meeting held last year, which was seen as a step forward in efforts to prevent emigration to the United States.
But in mid-September, senior administration officials made it clear that their intention was for the conference to go ahead, ending any consideration of sanctions for El Salvador.
Vice President Mike Pence plans to speak at the conference, which is now scheduled for mid-October and is seen as a sign of the administration’s importance to the meeting.
The three U.S. envoys quietly returned to El Salvador, Panama and the Dominican Republic without further threats or punishment from Washington.
A Bolton White House spokesman declined to comment on the details of the debate described by the three U.S. officials, including two diplomats, who agreed to discuss internal deliberations on condition of anonymity.
His explanations were corroborated by an external analyst close to the administration, who also spoke on condition of anonymity.
The Study of History
The planned next step could be special counsel Robert Mueller’s report on Trump’s possible obstruction of justice, of which there is already considerable evidence in the public record.
It is thought that Mueller is also carrying this one investigating the possibility that the Trump campaign would work in parallel with Russia in its attack on our elections.
If Congress changes hands, Trump will be held accountable to that chamber, just as he prepares to stand again before voters, and perhaps finally before a jury of his peers.
This is a lot of conditions, and with it I don’t suggest that Trump’s fall is unavoidable, nor that of its equivalents in Europe.
We will all have to make decisions on both sides of the Atlantic that will have an effect on the duration of the contest.
In 1938, the German officers were ready to strike a coup d'état against Hitler. It was only necessary that the West had resisted him and supported the Czechoslovaks in Munich.
We failed and lost the opportunity to avoid the years of carnage that followed.
The course of history revolves around these turning points, and the inexorable march of democracy is accelerated or delayed.
Americans now face several of these turning points.
What will we do if Trump dismisses Deputy Attorney General Rod Rosenstein, the man who controls the fate of the Mueller investigation?
Rosenstein has been seen in a good one since this newspaper reported that last year he had suggested secretly recording the president and had speculated about his incapacity for office.
Rosenstein says The Times explanation is not accurate.
“How will we respond if the F.B.B.I.’s new investigation into Brett Kavanaugh is not complete or fair, or if his office is confirmed in the Supreme Court despite credible allegations of sexual assault and dishonest testimony?
In addition to all of this, will we vote in the mid-term elections a Congress that will be accountable to Trump?
If we fail these exams, we will encounter a long winter for democracy.
But, thanks to the lesson I learned in Prague, I don't think we'll fail.
My mother was a Czechoslovak Jew deported to Auschwitz by the same Nazi regime who had once occupied my ambassadorial house.
He managed to survive, immigrated to the United States and, 60 years later, sent me to link the Sabbath candles on the same table adorned with a swastika.
“With that inheritance, how can I not be optimistic about our future?”
Norman Eisen, professor emeritus of the Brookings Institution, is the president of Citizens for Responsibility and Ethics in Washington and author of “The Last Palace: Europe’s Turbulent Century in Five Lives and One Legendary House.”
Rangers' Graham Dorrans, optimist against the rapid Vienna match
The Rangers will play home on Thursday against Rapid Vienna, knowing that a victory over the Austrians, after Spain’s impressive draw against Villarreal earlier this month, will place them in a strong position to qualify in Group G of the European League.
A knee injury prevented midfielder Graham Dorrans from achieving their first appearance of the season until the 2-to-2 draw against Villarreal, but he believes the Rangers can use that result as a trampoline for bigger things.
“We managed to make a good point, since Villarreal is a good team,” the 31-year-old said.
“We started the game believing that we could get something and came out with one point.
Perhaps we could have achieved the victory in the end, but, in general, the draw was perhaps a fair result.
They were best in the first half, but after the break we left for all and were the best team.
This Thursday we will have another great European night.
I hope we can achieve all three points, but it will be a difficult match because they already got a good result in the previous game. But, with all the public supporting us, I'm sure we will be able to advance and get a positive result.
Last year it was definitely hard with my injuries and the changes in the club itself, but now you notice a general atmosphere that everything will go well.
The team is fine, the guys are enjoying a lot and we’re having a good workout.
I’m hopeful that we will be able to move forward, leave behind last season and achieve success.”
Fear of Retirement Savings Is Losing Some Women Sleep
Despite the fact that the survey participants had a clear idea of how they wanted to be treated, there are few people talking about this with their families.
About half of the people who participated in the Nationwide study said they did talk to their spouses about the cost of long-term care.
Only 10 percent said they talked to their children about it.
“People want a family member to take care of them, but they’re not taking steps to have this conversation,” Holly Snyder, vice president of life insurance company Nationwide, told us.
This is the way to get started.
Talk to your spouse and your children: you won’t be able to prepare your family to take care if you don’t express your wishes well in advance.
Work with your counselor and your family to talk about where and how to receive care, as those options can be a cost-determining factor.
Incorporate your financial advisor into the conversion, as it can also help you find a way to pay for those expenses.
Your long-term care financing options may include a traditional long-term care insurance policy, a cash-worthy hybrid life insurance policy to help you cover expenses or insure yourself with your own savings, as long as you have the money.
Negotiate your legal documents: get ahead of legal battles.
Take care of obtaining a power of health care to designate a trusted person to oversee their health care and to make sure that professionals fulfill their own wishes in case they are unable to communicate.
You can also prepare a power of attorney for your financies.
Select a trusted person to make financial decisions on your behalf and make sure that your bills are paid if you become incapacitated.
Don’t forget the small details: imagine that your elderly father has a medical emergency and is on his way to the hospital.
Would you be able to answer questions about medications and allergies?
Describe all of these details in a written plan so that it is always ready.
“It’s not just the financies that are at stake: what doctors do you have to deal with?” Martin asks.
“Which medicine are you taking?
Who will take care of the dog?
You have to have everything planned.”
A man with a carbine in Ilfracombe is shot several times
A man with a carbine was shot several times as he came home walking home after leaving at night.
The victim, a 40-year-old man, was in the Oxford Grove area of Ilfracombe (Devon) when he was shot in the chest, abdomen and hand.
Police officers described the shooting, which occurred around 02:30 (British time), as a “random act.”
The victim didn't see his attacker.
Their injuries are non-fatal and the police have made an appeal to potential witnesses.
Earthquakes and tsunamis in Indonesia
At least 384 people have been killed by a powerful earthquake and tsunami that hit the Indonesian city of Palu last Friday, authorities said. The death toll is expected to rise.
With blocked communications, relief officials have been unable to obtain any information from the Donggala regency, an area north of Palu that is closer to the epicenter of the 7.5-magnitude earthquake.
In Palu, more than 16,000 people were evacuated after the disaster.
Here you can see some important details about Palu and Donggala on Sulawesi Island:
Palu is the capital of Central Sulawesi province, which is located at the end of a narrow bay on the west coast of Sulawesi Island, with an estimated population of 379,800 in 2017.
The city was celebrating its 40th anniversary when the earthquake and tsunami occurred.
Donggala is a regency that stretches for more than 300 km (180 miles) of coastline on the north-west of Sulawesi Island.
The regency, which is a hierarchically lower administrative region than the province, had an estimated population of 299,200 in 2017.
Fishing and agriculture are the pillars of the economy of Central Sulawesi province, especially in the coastal region of Donggala.
Nickel mines also have their importance in the province, but they are mainly concentrated in Morowali, on the opposite coast of Sulawesi.
Palu and Donggala have been impacted by tsunamis several times over the past 100 years, according to the Indonesian Disaster Mitigation Agency.
In 1938, a tsunami killed more than 200 people and destroyed hundreds of houses in Donggala.
A tsunami also hit western Donggala in 1996, with nine fatalities.
Indonesia is located in the Pacific Fire Belt, an area of high seismic activity, and often suffers from earthquakes frequently.
Here we summarize some of the main earthquakes and tsunamis in recent years:
2004: A large earthquake on the western coast of the northern Sumatran province of Aceh in northern Sumatra caused a tsunami that hit 14 countries on December 26 and killed 226,000 people off the coast of the Indian Ocean. More than half of the victims occurred in Aceh.
2005: A series of strong earthquakes hit the western coast of Sumatra in late March and early April.
There were hundreds of fatalities on the island of Nias, off the coast of Sumatra.
2006: A 6.8-magnitude quake ravaged southern Java, Indonesia’s most populous island, and triggered a tsunami that hit the southern coast, with nearly 700 fatalities.
2009: A 7.6-magnitude quake struck near Padang city in West Sumatran province.
More than 1,100 people were killed.
2010: A 7.5-magnitude quake struck one of the Mentawai Islands near Sumatra, triggering a tsunami of up to 10 meters that shattered dozens of villages and killed about 300 people.
2016: A shallow earthquake hit Pidie Jaya’s regency in Aceh, causing destruction and panic by recalling the devastation of the deadly 2004 earthquake and tsunami.
This time did not cause any tsunamis, but the collapse of buildings caused the death of more than 100 people.
2018: The tourist island of Lombok (Indonesia) had a series of large-magnified quakes that killed more than 500 people, mostly on the north side of the island.
The quake devastated thousands of buildings and thousands of tourists were temporarily evicted.
Sarah Palin's eldest son arrested on domestic violence charges
Track Palin, the eldest son of former Alaska governor and vice-presidential candidate Sarah Palin, has been arrested on assault charges.
Palin, a 29-year-old citizen of Wasilla (Alaska), was arrested on suspicion of domestic violence, interfering with a report of domestic violence and resisting arrest, according to the report released Saturday by Alaskan state police.
According to that police report, when an acquaintance of hers tried to call the police to report the alleged crimes, he took her phone.
Palin has been sentenced to pretrial detention at Mat-Su Pretrial Facility and is being held with an unsecured bail of 500 dollars, KTUU reported.
He appeared in court Saturday where he pleaded “definitely innocent” when asked for his statement, the network said.
Palin faces three Class A misdemeanors, which means he could spend up to one year in prison and be fined 250,000 dollars.
He has also been charged with a Class B misdemeanor, with a one-day sentence in jail and a fine of 2,000 dollars.
This isn’t the first time criminal charges have been filed against Palin.
In December 2017, he was accused of assaulting his father, Todd Palin.
His mother, Sarah Palin, called the police to report the alleged attack.
The case is currently before the Alaska Veterans Court.
In January 2016, he was charged with domestic assault, interfering in reporting a crime of domestic violence and possessing a gun intoxicated, in connection with the incident.
His girlfriend had claimed he punched him in the face.
Several groups of veterans criticized Sarah Palin in 2016 for linking her son’s violent behavior to post-traumatic stress disorder stemming from her service in Iraq.
Earthquake and Tsunamium in Indonesia: Hundreds of Fatalities
At least 384 people were killed after an earthquake struck the Indonesian island of Sulawesi last Friday.
The 7.5-magnified quake triggered a tsunami and destroyed thousands of houses.
Electricity and communications networks have fallen and the death toll is expected to rise in the coming days.
The quake occurred right next to central Sulawesi, which lies northeast of Jakarta, Indonesia’s capital.
There are several videos that circulate on social networks and that show the moment of impact.
Hundreds of people had gathered for a beach festival in the city of Palu when the tsunami hit the coast.
Federal prosecutors exceptionally seek the death penalty for suspected terrorist attack in New York
New York federal prosecutors are seeking capital punishment for Sayfullo Saipov, the New York City terrorist attack suspect who killed eight people, an exceptional punishment that has not been executed in the state for a federal crime since 1953.
Saipov, 30, allegedly used a Home Depot rent pickup to carry out an attack on a bike lane on Lower Manhattan's West Side, shooting down pedestrians and cyclists he was on his way.
To justify a death penalty, prosecutors must prove that Saipov “intentionally” killed the eight victims and caused “intentionally” serious bodily injury, according to the notice of the death penalty application intent, filed in the Southern District of New York.
The two charges carry a possible death penalty, according to the court document.
Several weeks after the attack, a federal grand jury filed a 22-count indictment that included eight counts of murder with organized crime aid, which federal prosecutors often use in these cases, and a charge of motorized vehicle violence and destruction.
The attack required “substantial planning and premeditation,” according to prosecutors, who described how Saipov carried it out as “atrocious, cruel and depraved.”
“Sayfullo Habibullaevic Saipov caused injuries, damages and losses to the families and friends of Diego Enrique Angelini, Nicholas Cleves, Ann-Laure Decadt, Darren Drake, Ariel Erlij, Hernan Ferruchi, Hernan Diego Mendoza and Alejandro Damian Pagnucco,” the notice of intent said.
Five of the victims were tourists from Argentina.
It’s already been a decade since the last time the Southern District of New York prosecuted a death penalty case.
Khalid Barnes was found guilty of murdering two drug traffickers, but was eventually sentenced to life in prison in September 2009.
The last time a death penalty was executed in a New York federal case was Julius and Ethel Rosenbeng in 1953. This marriage was executed after they were convicted of conspiracy to commit espionage for the Soviet Union during the Cold War two years earlier.
Two Rosenbergs were executed in the electric chair on June 19, 1953.
Saipov, an immigrant originally from Uzbekistan, demonstrated his lack of remorse during the days and months following the attack, according to court documents.
According to police, he told investigators that he felt good about what he had done.
According to the indictment, Saipov told authorities that for his attack he was inspired by watching ISIS videos on his phone.
He also requested that the ISIS flag be raised in his hospital room, police said.
He has pleaded not guilty to the 22-count indictment.
David Patton, one of federal public defense attorneys representing Saipov, said they are “obviously disappointed” with the prosecution’s decision.
“We believe that the decision to seek the death penalty rather than accepting a guilty plea with a life sentence without the possibility of release will only prolong the trauma of these events for all people involved,” Patton said.
Saipov’s defense team had previously asked prosecutors not to ask for the death penalty.
Deputy Tory says NIGEL FARAGE should handle Brexit negotiations
Nigel Farage vowed to “mobilize the People’s Army” today during a protest at the Tory conference.
The former UKIP leader said politicians had to “feel the heat” of Eurosceptics, while one of Theresa May’s MPs suggested that he should deal with negotiations with the EU.
