Elite League: Dundee Stars 5-3 Belfast Giants
Patrick Dwyer scored two goals for the Giants against Dundee
The Dundee Stars repaired an Elite League loss to the Belfast Giants on Friday by winning the second leg on Saturday 5–3 at Dundee.
The Giants achieved an initial two-goal lead thanks to attacks by Patrick Dwyer and Francis Beauvillier.
Mike Sullivan and Jordan Cownie won the home draw before Dwyer restored the Giants leadership.
Francois Bouchard equalized for Dundee before Lukas Lundvald Nielsen’s two goals secured their victory.
This has been Adam Keefe's third loss of the Elite League season, which had beaten Dundee 2-1 in Belfast on Friday night.
It was the fourth game of the season between the two teams, of which the Giants won the previous three games.
Dwyer's first goal came in the fourth minute (3:35) thanks to assistance from Kendall McFaull, while David Rutherford provided assistance for Beauvillier to double the lead four minutes later.
In what can be considered a very lively initial period, Sullivan returned to put the home team with a desire to play in the 13:10 minute before Matt Marquardt attended Cownie to equalize the game in the 15:16 minute.
Dwyer made sure the Giants took the lead ahead of the first break when he scored his second goal of the night at the end of the first half.
The home team regrouped and Bouchard once again equalized the match with a goal at 27:37.
Cownie and Charles Corcoran combined to help Nielsen give Dundee the lead for the first time in the game when the end of the second half came and secured the victory with his team's fifth goal as he approached the end.
The Giants, who have now lost four of their last five games, play at home against Milton Keynes in their next game on Friday.
An air traffic controller dies so that hundreds of people can escape on the plane of the earthquake
An Indonesian air traffic controller is being hailed as a hero after dying after making sure that a plane carrying hundreds of people managed to take off safely.
More than 800 people were killed and many missing after a large-scale earthquake struck Sulawesi Island on Friday, causing a tsunami.
Strong aftershocks continue to plague the area and many find themselves trapped among the rubble in the city of Palu.
Although his companions fleed to save their lives, Anthonius Gunawan Agung, 21, refused to leave his post at the Mutiara Sis Al Jufri Airport control tower.
He stood firm to make sure Batik Air Flight 6321, which was on the runway at the time, could take off securely.
He then jumped out of the traffic control tower when he thought it was collapsing.
He later died in the hospital.
Air Navigation Indonesia spokesman Yohannes Sirait said the decision could have saved hundreds of lives, Australian channel ABC News reported.
We had prepared a helicopter from Balikpapan in Kalimantan to take it to a larger hospital in another city.
Sadly, he died this morning before the helicopter arrived in Palu.
“Our hearts are broken by hearing this,” he added.
In the meantime, authorities fear that the death toll could reach several thousand people. The national disaster mitigation agency claims that access to Donggala, Sigi and Boutong cities is limited.
“It is believed that the number of victims will continue to increase, since there were still many corpses under the ruins and it has not been possible to contact a lot of people,” Sutopo Purwo Nugroho, a spokeswoman for the agency, said.
Waves that reached up to six meters have devastated Palu, which will hold a massive burial on Sunday.
Aid and supplies are being on military and commercial aircraft.
Risa Kusuma, a 35-year-old mother, told Sky News: “Every minute an ambulance with more bodies arrives.
Clean water is scarce.
Mini-markets are being looted everywhere.”
Jan Gelfand, head of the International Red Cross in Indonesia, told CNN: “The Indonesian Red Cross is trying to help survivors as much as possible, but we don’t know what they’ll find there
This is already a tragedy, but it could have been much worse.”
Indonesian President Joko Widodo arrived in Palu on Sunday and told the country’s military: “I ask everyone to work day and night to complete all evacuation-related tasks.
“Are you ready?” CNN reported.
Indonesia had already been whipped earlier this year by earthquakes in Lombok in which more than 550 people were killed.
