North Korea’s defector figures “fell” with Kim
North Korea’s number of defectors to South Korea has declined since Kim Jong-un came to power seven years ago, a South Korean lawmaker has said.
Park Byeong-seug, citing data from the Southern Unification Ministry, said that last year there were 1,127 defections, compared to 2,706 in 2011.
Park said tighter border controls between North Korea and China, and the high fees charged by human traffickers have been the key factors.
Pyongyang has made no public comments about it.
The vast majority of deserters in the north are finally offered South Korean citizenship.
Seoul says more than 30,000 North Koreans have illegally crossed the border since the end of the Korean War in 1953.
Most flee through China, which has the longest border with North Korea and is easier to cross than the demilitarized zone (DMZ) heavily protected between the two Koreas.
China considers defectors to be illegable rather than refugees and are often repatriated.
Relations between the North and the South, which are still technically at war, have improved markedly in recent months.
Earlier this month, the leaders of the two countries gathered in Pyongyang to have talks that focused on denuclearization negotiations.
This came after the historic meeting in June between U.S. President Donald Trump and Kim Jong-un in Singapore, who generally agreed to work for a nuclear-weapon-free Korean peninsula.
But on Saturday, North Korea’s Foreign Minister Ri Yong-ho blamed U.S. sanctions on lack of progress.
“Without U.S. confidence, there will be no confidence in our national security and, under these circumstances, in no way will we be the first to disarm us unilaterally,” Ri said in a speech to the U.N. General Assembly in New York.
Nancy Pelosi calls Brett Kavanaugh “hysterical” and says he is unfitable to serve in the Supreme Court
Nancy Pelosi, House minority leader, said Supreme Court nominee Brett Kavanaugh was a “hysterical” and was unsuitable to serve in the Supreme Court.
Pelosi made these comments in an interview Saturday at the Texas Tribune Festival in Austin, Texas.
“I can’t help but think that if a woman had acted that way, they would have said she’s hysterical,” Pelosi said of her reaction to Kavanaugh’s testimony Thursday before the Senate Judiciary Committee.
Kavanaugh used the excitement to deny the accusations he had sexually assaulted Dr. Christine Blasey Ford when the two were teenagers.
During his initial statement, Kavanaugh was very excited, sometimes almost screaming and choking while talking about his family and his high school years.
In the committee, he also explicitly condemned the Democrats, calling the accusations against him “grotesque and clearly coordinated defamations” organized by angry liberals because Hillary Clinton lost the 2016 presidential election.
Pelosi said that, in his view, Kavanaugh’s testimony proved that he could not serve in the Supreme Court, because he showed his bias against the Democrats.
“I think he disqualifies himself with those statements and the way he’s persecuted the Clintons and the Democrats,” he said.
Pelosi voiced when asked if he would challenge Kavanaugh in the event that he was confirmed in office and that the Democrats held a majority in the House of Representatives.
“I’ll just say one thing: if you’re not telling the truth to Congress or the FBI, it won’t be fit not just to be in the Supreme Court, it won’t even be to be in the court where you exercise now,” Pelosi said.
Kavanaugh is currently a judge in the Washington D.C. Circuit Court of Appeals.
