There was a moment this morning when we heated the situation in Europe.
But we're losing him.
I'd like to congratulate Thomas.
He's a great captain.
Its 12 players have played a very good game.
We'll regroup, meet with the PGA of America and our Ryder Cup Committee, and we'll move forward.
I love these 12 guys a lot and I'm proud to be their captain.
Now it's our turn to bow our heads.
They've surpassed us."
Red Tide Update: Pinellas, Manatee and Sarasota Lowers
The new report by the Florida Fish and Wildlife Commission shows an overall reduction in red tide concentrations in areas of the Tampa Bay area.
According to the FWC, irregular flowering conditions are being reported in areas of Pinellas, Manatee, Sarasota, Charlotte and Collier counties, suggesting reduced concentrations.
Red tide flowering stretches approximately 130 miles of coastline from Pinellas County to the north to Lee County to the south.
Irregular plots can be found about 10 miles off the coast of Hillsborough County, but in fewer places compared to last week.
Red tide has also been detected in Pasco County.
Over the past week, average concentrations have been reported in Pinellas County and its coast, low- and high-cost concentrations on the Hillsborough County coast, bottom-to-high concentrations in Manatee County, background-to-high concentrations in Sarasota County and its coastline, bottom-to-medium concentrations in Charlotte County, bottom-to-high concentrations in Lee County and on its coast
Respiratory irritation issues continue to be reported in Pinellas, Manatee, Sarasota, Lee and Collier counties.
Respiratory irritation problems have not been reported in northwestern Florida over the past week.
